/* Sessions 25–30 (Weeks 14–16) + Final unit (Week 17). Content only. */
COURSE.sessions.push(
/* ================= WEEK 14 ================= */
{ id:25, week:14, unit:'Type checking & scope', ic:'🧩', lo:[4], book:'Dragon Ch. 5.5, 6.5',
  title:'Type Checking in Practice and Implementing L-Attributed SDDs',
  sub:'Type conversions as real instructions, checking function calls and returns, scope across phases, and turning an L-attributed SDD into a recursive-descent translator that emits code on the fly.',
  introH:'From rules on paper to a working translator',
  intro:'We already know <i>what</i> to check (types and scopes) and <i>which</i> SDDs are easy to evaluate. Today we write the real code: a recursive-descent parser that checks types, inserts conversions and writes out instructions while it parses.',
  introAn:'Until now we studied the traffic rules. Today we sit in the driving seat and actually drive: every rule must turn into an action of the hands and feet.',
  introN:{say:'Write <code>float y; int k; y = k * 2 + 1.5;</code> on the board. Ask: “Can the CPU add an int and a float directly?” Collect guesses. Promise that by the end of the class they will see the exact instructions the compiler produces for this line, including a hidden conversion.', ask:'Is this lecture about type checking or about parsing?', ans:'Both at once. The parser is the skeleton; type checking and code generation are actions that run inside the parser’s functions.', watch:'Students may think type checking is a separate pass after parsing. In many real compilers it is, but today we show the one-pass version, which is simpler to build.'},
  obj:['Generate code for type conversions during translation.','Check function calls (argument count and types) and return statements.','Implement L-attributed SDDs in recursive descent with parameters and return values.','Generate code on the fly instead of building long code strings.','Describe how attributes are kept on an LL parser stack.'],
  bloom:{head:['<b>Apply</b> type rules and insert conversion code.','<b>Implement</b> L-attributed SDDs as recursive-descent functions.','<b>Compare</b> building code strings with emitting code on the fly.'],
    heart:['<b>Value</b> an efficient, simple translator design.','<b>Care</b> about correct and helpful type error messages.'],
    hand:['<b>Code</b> a translator that emits three-address code with conversions.','<b>Test</b> function-call checks with good and bad calls.']},
  slides:[
    {k:'Concept', h:'Where we are: check and translate in one pass', ic:'🧭', p:'Our parser already knows the structure of the program. Now we add actions to its functions so that, while it parses, it also checks the meaning and writes out <b>three-address code</b> (simple instructions with one operator each, like <code>t1 = k * 2</code>; Session 26 covers them fully).', pts:[['🔍','<b>Check:</b> do the types fit together?'],['🔁','<b>Convert:</b> add a conversion where an int meets a float.'],['✍️','<b>Write:</b> produce the instructions as we go.']], an:'A cricket scorer checks each ball (wide? no-ball?) and writes it in the scorebook at once, instead of watching the whole match and writing everything at the end.', tk:'One pass of the parser can parse, check and translate together.',
      n:{say:'Draw the front end as three boxes: scanner → parser → (type checker + code writer). Then draw a big circle around the last two and the parser: today they work together, inside the parser’s functions.', ask:'Why not check types after the whole tree is built?', ans:'You can, and big compilers often do. One pass is smaller, faster to write and enough for a simple language like the one in our labs.', watch:'Three-address code appears today before its full lecture. Keep it simple: one operator per line, temporaries t1, t2… hold the in-between results.'}},
    {k:'Comparison', h:'Two kinds of type conversion', ic:'🔄', p:'A <b>type conversion</b> changes a value from one type to another. If the compiler does it on its own, it is a <b>coercion</b>. If the programmer writes it, like <code>(int) x</code>, it is a <b>cast</b>.', cmp:[['⬆️ Widening (safe)',['int → float, int → double','Nothing important is lost: 3 becomes 3.0','The compiler does it on its own (coercion)']],['⬇️ Narrowing (risky)',['float → int, double → int','The fraction is lost: 3.7 becomes 3','Usually needs a cast written by the programmer']]], an:'Pouring water from a small glass into a big jug is safe (widening). Pouring a full jug into a small glass spills some water (narrowing).', tk:'Widening happens automatically; narrowing needs a cast.',
      n:{say:'Show <code>float f = 3;</code> (fine, becomes 3.0) and <code>int n = 3.7;</code>. In Java the second line is a compile error; in C it compiles and n becomes 3, silently. Then show <code>int n = (int) 3.7;</code>, which both languages accept.', ask:'Is int → float always perfectly safe?', ans:'Almost. A float has only about 7 exact digits, so a very large int such as 123456789 becomes 123456792 as a float. For normal numbers it is exact.', watch:'Students mix up the words: <b>widening</b> = to a bigger type (safe), <b>narrowing</b> = to a smaller type (loses data). Coercion = automatic; cast = written by hand.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Why a conversion is a real instruction', ic:'🧮', p:'The int 2 and the float 2.0 are stored as completely different bit patterns. The CPU also has separate circuits for integer and floating-point arithmetic. So before adding an int to a float, the compiler must emit a real instruction that converts the int.', code:`int   2    →  00000000 00000000 00000000 00000010
float 2.0  →  01000000 00000000 00000000 00000000

t1 = (float) i      // the conversion instruction we emit`, an:'100 rupees and 100 dollars are not the same money. You must go to the exchange counter and convert before you add them up.', tk:'A coercion is not free: it becomes an instruction in the output.',
      n:{say:'Point at the two bit patterns: the same value 2, but nothing in common. On x86 the instruction <code>t1 = (float) i</code> becomes <code>cvtsi2ss</code> (convert signed integer to single float). Students can see it at godbolt.org by compiling <code>float f(int i){ return i * 2.5f; }</code>.', ask:'Could the compiler just reuse the int bits as a float?', ans:'No. Reading the int bits of 2 as a float gives a tiny number close to 0 (about 2.8 × 10⁻⁴⁵), not 2.0.', watch:'Students think “int and float are both numbers, so the CPU just adds them”. Stress that the hardware cannot mix them.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'The widening ladder and max()', ic:'🪜', p:'Types are arranged on a ladder from narrow to wide. When two operands meet, the result takes the <b>wider</b> type. The helper <code>max(t1, t2)</code> returns whichever of the two types is higher on the ladder.', code:`char → int → long → float → double      (narrow → wide)

max(int, int)      = int
max(int, float)    = float
max(float, double) = double`, an:'Two friends share a taxi: if one wants to go 5 km and the other 10 km, the taxi must go the longer distance, 10 km.', tk:'The result type of a mixed operation is the wider of the two types.',
      n:{say:'Draw the ladder on the board as stairs. Ask: where does int + double land? (double). Explain that this ladder is Java’s widening order; C has a very similar order called the “usual arithmetic conversions”.', ask:'Why is long below float? A long has 64 bits and a float only 32.', ans:'Because a float can hold much larger values (up to about 3.4 × 10³⁸), even though it is less precise. The ladder is about range, so long → float counts as widening.', watch:'max() here compares types, not numbers. max(int, float) is the type float, not a number.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'The widen() helper and the rule for +', ic:'🛠️', p:'<code>widen</code> takes an address, its type and the type we need. If they match, it does nothing. If an int must become a float, it emits a conversion into a new temporary. <b>E.addr</b> is the name or temporary that holds the value of E.', code:`Addr widen(Addr a, Type t, Type w) {     // a has type t; we need type w
    if (t == w) return a;                 // same type: no code at all
    if (t == integer &amp;&amp; w == float) {
        Temp tmp = newtemp();
        gen(tmp + " = (float) " + a);     // emit the conversion
        return tmp;
    }
    error("type error");                  // e.g. float → int is not widening
}
E → E1 + E2  { E.type = max(E1.type, E2.type);
               a1 = widen(E1.addr, E1.type, E.type);
               a2 = widen(E2.addr, E2.type, E.type);
               E.addr = newtemp();  gen(E.addr " = " a1 " + " a2); }`, an:'A plug adapter: if the plug already fits the socket, you plug it straight in; if not, you put an adapter in between.', tk:'Widen both operands to the result type, then operate.',
      n:{say:'Trace <code>i + 2.5</code> with i an int. E.type = max(int, float) = float. widen(i, int, float) emits <code>t1 = (float) i</code> and returns t1. widen(2.5, float, float) returns 2.5 unchanged. Then <code>t2 = t1 + 2.5</code>.', ask:'Why call widen on both operands, if only one of them needs it?', ans:'We do not know in advance which side is the int. Calling it on both is simple, and when no conversion is needed widen emits nothing.', watch:'A common bug: converting only the left operand. Real code must work for <code>2.5 + i</code> as well as <code>i + 2.5</code>.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Checking x = i * 2.5', demo:'typecheck', an:'A shopkeeper who checks every note and converts foreign money before putting it in the till.', tk:'The coercion appears as t1 = (float) i.',
      n:{say:'Press Step row by row. At row 3 stop and ask: which operand gets converted, and to what? (i, to float). At the last row show that a string cannot be widened to float, so the checker reports an error.', ask:'Why does 2.5 not need a conversion?', ans:'2.5 is already a float; the lexer gave it that type when it read the literal.', watch:'Students sometimes write <code>t1 = (float) 2.5</code>. Only the int side is converted.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'Worked example: y = k * 2 + 1.5', ic:'✍️', p:'Here <code>y</code> is a float and <code>k</code> is an int. Work from the innermost operator outwards. The conversion is inserted exactly where an int first meets a float, not earlier.', code:`float y;  int k;
y = k * 2 + 1.5;

k * 2       int × int     → int      t1 = k * 2
t1 + 1.5    int + float   → float    t2 = (float) t1
                                     t3 = t2 + 1.5
y = t3      float = float  ✓         y = t3`, an:'You add up a bill in rupees first, and only at the very end convert the total to dollars, instead of converting every item.', tk:'k * 2 stays integer arithmetic; only its result is widened.',
      n:{say:'Do this one together on the board, line by line, asking the class for each type. Then change it to <code>y = k * 2;</code>: now the assignment itself needs the conversion: <code>t1 = k * 2</code>, <code>t2 = (float) t1</code>, <code>y = t2</code>.', ask:'Would it be wrong to convert k first and multiply in float?', ans:'The answer would usually be the same, but it is not what the language rules say and it uses slower float arithmetic. The compiler must follow the rules exactly.', watch:'Students forget that assigning an int value to a float variable is also a widening, which needs its own conversion instruction.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Checking a function call', ic:'📞', p:'A function also has a type, written as a <b>function type</b>: the parameter types, an arrow, and the <b>return type</b>. For a call, look the function up, check the number of arguments, check each argument type (widening allowed), and give the call the return type.', code:`float f(int a, float b);       // type: int × float → float
float x;
x = f(2, 3);

t1 = (float) 3       // argument 3 is int; parameter b wants float
param 2
param t1
t2 = call f, 2       // 2 = number of arguments
x = t2`, an:'A courier counter checks your parcel: the right number of boxes, each box the right size. A slightly small box is fine (widening); an oversized one is rejected.', tk:'Calls are checked for count, argument types and result type.',
      n:{say:'Point at the type <code>int × float → float</code>: read it as “takes an int and a float, gives back a float”. Then walk the TAC: <code>param</code> passes each argument, <code>call f, 2</code> jumps to f with 2 arguments. Show two bad calls: <code>f(2)</code> (too few arguments) and <code>f("hi", 3)</code> (string cannot become int).', ask:'Does a real compiler emit <code>t1 = (float) 3</code> for a constant?', ans:'A smart one simply writes the constant 3.0, because it can do the conversion at compile time (constant folding). For a variable argument the run-time conversion is needed.', watch:'Students check only the number of arguments and forget the types, or the other way round.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Checking return statements and void', ic:'↩️', p:'For <code>return E;</code> the type of E must fit the return type of the function we are inside. That information comes from the function header above, so it is passed <b>down</b> into the body as an <b>inherited attribute</b>. A void function has no value, so it cannot be used inside an expression.', code:`float f(int a, float b) { return a * b; }   // int × float → float  ✓
int   g()               { return 2.5; }     // float → int: narrowing  ✘
void  h();
x = h() + 1;                                // void has no value  ✘`, an:'A waiter brings a dish and checks it against the order slip from the table. The slip comes from earlier (the order), not from the kitchen.', tk:'The expected return type is inherited from the function header.',
      n:{say:'For g: Java reports a compile error, while C converts 2.5 to 2 silently (gcc warns only with <code>-Wconversion</code>). For h: gcc says “void value not ignored as it ought to be”.', ask:'Why can the return type not be a synthesized attribute of the return statement?', ans:'Synthesized attributes come up from the children, here the expression E. The declared type is not inside E; it is in the function header, so it must come down from above.', watch:'Students check the return against the wrong function in nested or recursive code. Each function passes down its own return type.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Helpful type error messages', ic:'🩺', p:'Finding a type error is only half the job. A good message says where it is, what was expected and what was found. Then the checker should carry on, without printing ten more errors caused by the first one.', pts:[['📍','Give the line and column of the error.'],['⚖️','Say expected vs found: “expected 2 arguments, found 1”.'],['🔇','Give a bad expression a special “error” type, so later checks stay quiet about it.'],['➡️','Keep checking the rest of the program, like panic-mode recovery does for syntax.']], an:'A good teacher writes “line 3: you used + where the formula needs ×” in the margin, not just a red cross on the whole page.', tk:'Precise messages and no error avalanche.',
      n:{say:'Show two real messages. gcc: <code>error: too few arguments to function ’f’</code>. javac: <code>error: incompatible types: possible lossy conversion from double to int</code>. Ask the class which one is more helpful and why.', ask:'Why give a broken expression an “error” type instead of stopping at once?', ans:'So the compiler can report other, independent errors in the same run. Any check involving the error type is skipped quietly, which avoids follow-up messages.', watch:'One real mistake often produces a cascade of messages. Teach students to always fix the <b>first</b> error first.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Scope across the phases', ic:'🗂️', pts:[['🔤','<b>Lexer:</b> sees only the lexeme <code>x</code>; it knows nothing about scope.'],['🌳','<b>Parser + actions:</b> open a scope at {, insert at each declaration, look up at each use, close the scope at }.'],['🧾','<b>Code generator:</b> uses the entry that lookup found, so two different x’s get two different memory places.']], demo:'scopes', an:'Two students both called Ali, one in class A and one in class B. The register for each class tells you which Ali a teacher means.', tk:'After lookup, every use points to the right declaration.',
      n:{say:'Run the animation. At the row <code>{ float x;</code> stop: the inner x hides the outer one. At <code>x = 1.5; }</code> ask which x is meant (the float one). After the block closes, x means the int again.', ask:'Does the inner x overwrite the outer x’s value?', ans:'No. They are two different variables with two different symbol-table entries and two different memory places. The outer one is only hidden for a while.', watch:'Students think scope is handled by the lexer because the lexer puts names in the symbol table. Scope decisions need the block structure, which only the parser knows.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Recipe: from L-attributed SDD to code', ic:'📜', p:'Any <b>L-attributed</b> SDD (inherited values come only from the parent or from siblings on the left) can be turned into a <b>recursive-descent</b> translator with four simple rules.', pts:[['🧩','One function per non-terminal A.'],['⬇️','The inherited attributes of A become the <b>parameters</b> of A().'],['⬆️','The synthesized attributes of A become its <b>return value</b>.'],['📝','Inside A(): compute a child’s inherited values just before calling it; compute A’s synthesized values at the end.']], an:'A manager gives an instruction sheet to a worker (parameters going down); the worker returns a finished report (return value going up).', tk:'Inherited = parameters in; synthesized = return value out.',
      n:{say:'Draw a function box with an arrow going in (parameter = inherited) and an arrow coming out (return = synthesized). This is the same SDT-to-code rule from Session 23, now used for real.', ask:'What if a non-terminal has two synthesized attributes, e.g. addr and type?', ans:'Return a small record or struct with both fields, or use output (reference) parameters.', watch:'This only works for L-attributed SDDs. If a child needs a value from a sibling on its <b>right</b>, that value does not exist yet when we call the child.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'The recipe on a declaration: float a, b, c', ic:'🏷️', code:`D → T L        { L.inh = T.type }
L → id , L1    { addType(id.entry, L.inh);  L1.inh = L.inh }
L → id         { addType(id.entry, L.inh) }

void D()         { Type t = T();   L(t); }      // T.type goes up, then down into L
void L(Type inh) { Entry e = match(ID);  addType(e, inh);
                   if (look == ',') { match(',');  L(inh); } }`, demo:'inherit', an:'A teacher announces “all of you are in Section B” once; each student in the row writes Section B on their own form.', tk:'The type is returned by T() and passed as a parameter to L().',
      n:{say:'Trace <code>float a, b, c</code>: T() returns float; L(float) records a as float, sees a comma, calls L(float) for b, then for c. Then play the animation: the same flow of the inherited type, drawn as a tree.', ask:'Why is the grammar written L → id , L1 here, while the animation’s tree grows the other way?', ans:'The animation uses the book’s left-recursive grammar. Recursive descent cannot handle left recursion, so we use the right-recursive version. Both give every name the same type.', watch:'Here <code>match(ID)</code> also returns the identifier’s symbol-table entry. Point this out, or students will ask where e comes from.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'A translator for while: version 1 builds strings', ic:'🔁', p:'S gets the label of what comes after it (S.next, inherited) and returns its code (synthesized). The condition C gets two labels: where to jump when true and when false.', code:`string S(label next) {                 // next: inherited;  returns S.code
  if (look == WHILE) {
    match(WHILE);  match('(');
    label L1 = newlabel(), L2 = newlabel();
    string Ccode  = C(L2, next);       // C.true = L2,  C.false = S.next
    match(')');
    string S1code = S(L1);             // after the body, go back to L1
    return "label " + L1 + Ccode
         + "label " + L2 + S1code + "goto " + L1;
  } ...
}`, an:'Building a train by joining coaches: test coach, body coach, then a sign “back to the start”.', tk:'Labels are inherited; code is synthesized.',
      n:{say:'Read the return line slowly: label L1 (top of loop), the test, label L2 (start of body), the body, goto L1 (back to the test). If the test is false, C jumps to next and the loop ends.', ask:'Why does S1 receive L1 as its next label?', ans:'When the body finishes, control must go back to the test at L1, not fall into whatever follows the loop.', watch:'This version copies long strings again and again: the body’s code is copied into the loop’s code, which is copied into its parent’s code, and so on. The next slide fixes that.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'Version 2: emit code on the fly', ic:'⚡', p:'If the code of a construct is just its children’s code <b>in the same order</b> with some new instructions between them, we do not need strings at all. We <b>emit</b> (write out) each instruction immediately. This is <b>on-the-fly code generation</b>.', code:`void S(label next) {                   // nothing returned: code is emitted
  if (look == WHILE) {
    match(WHILE);  match('(');
    label L1 = newlabel(), L2 = newlabel();
    emit("label " + L1);
    C(L2, next);  match(')');           // C emits its own jumps
    emit("label " + L2);
    S(L1);                              // the body emits its own code
    emit("goto " + L1);
  } ...
}`, an:'Instead of writing a whole letter in rough, then copying it neatly, an experienced writer writes the final copy straight away, line by line.', tk:'Emit in order: no string building, no copying.',
      n:{say:'Compare with version 1 side by side: every piece that was concatenated is now emitted at the same position. The order of emits is exactly the order of the old concatenation.', ask:'When can we NOT emit on the fly?', ans:'When the code must come out in a different order from the parse, for example if a construct needed its second child’s code before its first child’s. Then we must build pieces and reorder them, or use backpatching (Session 30).', watch:'Order matters: emit label L2 before calling S(L1), otherwise the label lands after the body and the true-jump goes to the wrong place.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'Real output: while (i < n) i = i + 1;', ic:'🖨️', p:'Follow the calls and watch the output grow. Assume the caller created the label Lnext for the statement after the loop.', code:`call / action             output
S(Lnext)                   (makes L1, L2)
emit label L1              L1:
C(L2, Lnext)                   if i &lt; n goto L2
                               goto Lnext
emit label L2              L2:
S(L1)  for i = i + 1           t1 = i + 1
                               i = t1
emit goto L1                   goto L1
caller emits Lnext         Lnext:`, an:'A tour guide who announces each stop the moment the bus reaches it, so the passengers’ notes are in the right order automatically.', tk:'Six instructions and three labels, produced in one left-to-right pass.',
      n:{say:'Trace it with i = 0, n = 2 on the board: L1 test true → L2 → i becomes 1 → goto L1 → test true → i becomes 2 → goto L1 → test false → Lnext. The loop ran twice.', ask:'Where does <code>goto Lnext</code> come from?', ans:'From C: a comparison emits <code>if i &lt; n goto C.true</code> and then <code>goto C.false</code>. Here C.false is Lnext, the label passed down from the loop.', watch:'Students forget the final goto L1 and get a loop that runs only once. Session 28 shows the same pattern with the full set of rules.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Translating with a table-driven LL parser', ic:'🗄️', p:'A table-driven <b>LL(1) parser</b> has no function calls to carry parameters, so it keeps the attribute values on its stack instead.', pts:[['📌','A non-terminal’s own stack record holds its <b>inherited</b> attributes.'],['⚙️','An <b>action record</b> sits just above a non-terminal (it is popped first) and computes that non-terminal’s inherited attributes.'],['📦','A <b>synthesize record</b> sits just below a non-terminal and collects its synthesized attributes when it is finished.'],['🔁','When a record is popped, it copies its values into the records lower down that need them.']], an:'A relay race: each runner hands a baton (values) to the next runner waiting further down the track.', tk:'The table-driven parser can translate too; it just keeps more on its stack.',
      n:{say:'Draw a stack with the top at the top: [action: L2 = newlabel()…] [C] [synthesize C] [ ) ] [action: S1.next = L1] [S1] [synthesize S1]. Pop from the top and say what each record does. Keep it at this picture level; exam questions usually ask for the idea, not a full trace.', ask:'Why must the action record be above the non-terminal, not below it?', ans:'The stack is popped from the top. The inherited values must be computed before the non-terminal is expanded, so the action has to be popped first.', watch:'Do not confuse an action record (parser stack) with an activation record (the run-time stack frame of a function call). Different topics.'}}
  ],
  lab:{t:'Lab: a typed translator', steps:['Extend your recursive-descent expression translator so each expression function returns two things: its address and its type.','Use <code>max()</code> and <code>widen()</code> to emit <code>(float)</code> conversions when int and float meet.','Test it on <code>y = k * 2 + 1.5;</code> with y float and k int. You should get <code>t1 = k * 2</code>, <code>t2 = (float) t1</code>, <code>t3 = t2 + 1.5</code>, <code>y = t3</code>.','Add function declarations and calls. Check the number and types of arguments and print a clear message for <code>f(2)</code> and <code>f("hi", 3)</code>.','Translate <code>float f(int a, float b) { return a * b; }</code> and <code>x = f(2, 3);</code> and print the three-address code.'], out:'Your code, the three-address code with conversions, and the error messages for the bad calls.',
    n:{say:'Give students the expression translator from the Session 23 lab as a starting point. Pairs: one writes widen(), the other writes the function-call check; then they merge.', ask:'How do I return both an address and a type from one function?', ans:'Return a small struct or class, e.g. <code>class Res { String addr; Type type; }</code>, or use two output parameters.', watch:'Check that students emit the conversion before the instruction that uses it. If <code>t2 = t1 + 1.5</code> appears before <code>t1 = (float) …</code>, the order of their emit calls is wrong.'}},
  disc:['Why are labels inherited and code synthesized?','When can on-the-fly emission not be used?','How does C++ choose between overloaded functions?'],
  mistakes:['Converting only one operand instead of both operands to the result type.','Checking the return type against the wrong function (missing inherited context).','Emitting the body S1 before the label that S1 needs.','Treating every type mismatch as an error even when widening is allowed.','Thinking int and float can be added without a conversion instruction.'],
  rev:['Coercions become explicit conversion instructions such as t1 = (float) i.','The result of a mixed operation takes the wider type: max(int, float) = float.','Function calls: check the count and types of the arguments; the expected return type is inherited from the function header.','L-attributed SDD in recursive descent: inherited = parameters, synthesized = return values.','On-the-fly emission avoids building long code strings; LL stacks use action and synthesize records instead.'],
  hw:'Read Dragon §6.1–6.2. Prepare for Quiz 4.',
  check:[
    {q:'For <code>float y; int k; y = k * 2 + 1.5;</code> where does the compiler put the conversion?', o:['Convert k before multiplying','Convert the result of k * 2 before adding 1.5','Convert 1.5 to int','No conversion is needed'], a:1, e:'k * 2 is int × int, so it stays an int. The conversion is needed only where that int result meets the float 1.5: t2 = (float) t1.'},
    {q:'Why is the expected type of a <code>return</code> statement an <b>inherited</b> attribute?', o:['Because return values always go up the tree','Because it comes from the function header above, not from the expression below','Because the lexer computes it','Because it is always int'], a:1, e:'The declared return type is written in the function header, so it must be passed down into the body. Synthesized values only come from children.'},
    {q:'In the on-the-fly while translator, what goes wrong if you call <code>S(L1)</code> before <code>emit("label " + L2)</code>?', o:['Nothing','The body code appears before label L2, so the true-jump skips the body','The loop runs twice','A syntax error is reported'], a:1, e:'On-the-fly code comes out in call order. L2 must mark the start of the body; if it is written after the body, jumping to L2 skips it.'}],
  quiz:[
    {q:'Mixing int i and float 2.5 in i * 2.5 generates…', o:['A syntax error','A conversion instruction t1 = (float) i','Nothing','A new token'], a:1, e:'Widening coercion: the int operand is converted to float first.'},
    {q:'In recursive descent, inherited attributes are…', o:['Return values','Parameters','Global variables only','Tokens'], a:1, e:'They are passed in to the function of the non-terminal.'},
    {q:'On-the-fly generation means…', o:['Emitting instructions as soon as they are known','Building the whole AST first','Interpreting','Linking'], a:0, e:'Each instruction is written out at once; no long code strings are built.'},
    {q:'A return statement’s expected type is best passed as…', o:['A synthesized attribute','An inherited attribute','A token','A comment'], a:1, e:'It comes from the enclosing function’s header.'},
    {q:'For f(int, float) called as f(2, 3), the compiler…', o:['Rejects it','Widens 3 to float','Narrows 2','Ignores types'], a:1, e:'int → float is a widening, so it is allowed automatically.'},
    {q:'max(int, float) in the type rules returns…', o:['int','float','The larger number','An error'], a:1, e:'max compares types on the widening ladder; float is wider than int.'},
    {q:'In a table-driven LL parser, the synthesized attributes of A are collected in…', o:['An action record above A','A synthesize record just below A','The lexer','The symbol table'], a:1, e:'The synthesize record sits below A and receives A’s results when A is finished.'}],
  terms:[['Type conversion','Changing a value from one type to another, implicitly (coercion) or explicitly (cast).'],['Widening','A conversion to a wider type, such as int → float, that keeps the value.'],['Narrowing','A conversion to a narrower type, such as float → int, that can lose data.'],['Function type','A type expression s → t from the argument types to the result type.'],['On-the-fly code generation','Emitting instructions during parsing instead of building code attributes.'],['Action record','An LL stack entry that computes inherited attributes.'],['Synthesize record','An LL stack entry that collects synthesized attributes.']]
},
{ id:26, week:14, unit:'Intermediate code generation', ic:'🧾', lo:[4], book:'Dragon Ch. 6.1–6.2 · Cooper & Torczon Ch. 5',
  title:'Intermediate Code Generation and Three-Address Code',
  sub:'Why compilers use an IR; syntax trees and DAGs; three-address code and its instruction forms; quadruples, triples and indirect triples; a first look at SSA.',
  assess:'Quiz-4',
  introH:'The language in the middle',
  intro:'The front end’s final product is an <b>intermediate representation</b>: a simple, in-between form of the program. The most common one is <b>three-address code</b>, made of tiny instructions like <code>t1 = b * c</code> that any back end can turn into machine code.',
  introAn:'At a United Nations meeting, an Urdu speech may be translated into English first, and from English into Chinese, French and Arabic. English is the middle language that saves hiring a translator for every pair.',
  introN:{say:'Write <code>x = a + b * c - d;</code> on the board. Ask: “If you could only do ONE arithmetic operation per line, how would you write this?” Let students try. Their answer is three-address code. Remind them Quiz 4 is this week (see the assessment slide at the end).', ask:'Is intermediate code ever run directly?', ans:'Sometimes. Java bytecode and Python bytecode are intermediate codes that a virtual machine runs. Usually, though, the IR is only used inside the compiler and then turned into machine code.', watch:'Students often think the compiler goes straight from the tree to machine code. Stress that almost every real compiler has at least one IR in the middle.'},
  obj:['Explain why compilers use intermediate representations.','Build syntax trees and DAGs for expressions.','Write three-address code using the standard instruction forms.','Represent three-address code as quadruples, triples and indirect triples.','Describe static single-assignment (SSA) form at a high level.'],
  bloom:{head:['<b>Explain</b> the role of an IR.','<b>Generate</b> three-address code for expressions.','<b>Compare</b> quadruples, triples and indirect triples.'],
    heart:['<b>Appreciate</b> the power of a common middle language.','<b>Value</b> simplicity in design.'],
    hand:['<b>Write</b> three-address code for given statements.','<b>Fill</b> quadruple and triple tables.']},
  slides:[
    {k:'Concept', h:'Why an intermediate representation?', ic:'🧾', p:'Suppose we want 3 languages (C, Java, Rust) on 4 kinds of machine. Without an IR we need 3 × 4 = 12 separate compilers. With one shared IR we need only 3 front ends + 4 back ends = 7 parts. Adding a new machine means writing one back end: this is <b>retargeting</b>.', pts:[['🔁','m front ends + n back ends instead of m × n compilers.'],['🚀','Machine-independent optimisation is written once, on the IR.'],['🪜','One big translation becomes two smaller, easier ones.']], an:'English as the middle language at the UN: each speech is translated into English once, then from English into every other language.', tk:'The IR is the contract between the front end and the back end.',
      n:{say:'Draw 3 language boxes on the left and 4 machine boxes on the right. First join every pair with a line (12 lines, messy). Then redraw with one IR box in the middle (7 lines). Real example: LLVM IR is shared by clang (C/C++), Rust and Swift, and has back ends for x86, ARM, RISC-V and more.', ask:'Does the IR make the compiler slower?', ans:'A little, because of the extra step, but it makes good optimisation possible, so the final program is usually faster. Compile time is a small price.', watch:'Students think IR is only for big compilers. Even small teaching compilers use one because it makes each part simpler to write and test.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'High-level to low-level IR', demo:'irforms', an:'A building plan goes from an architect’s sketch (high level), to detailed drawings, to the builder’s brick-by-brick list (low level).', tk:'Trees for analysis; three-address code for optimisation and code generation.',
      n:{say:'Step through the four forms. Stress the direction: as we move right, the form is closer to the machine and further from the source. A compiler may use several of these one after another.', ask:'Which form keeps the brackets and structure of the source?', ans:'The syntax tree. Three-address code has no brackets at all; the order of the lines replaces them.', watch:'Remind students that a syntax tree (AST) is not the same as a parse tree: it leaves out helper non-terminals and punctuation.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'A DAG shares repeated work', demo:'dag', p:'A <b>DAG</b> is a syntax tree in which an identical subexpression is stored only <b>once</b> and shared by all its users. Here <code>b − c</code> appears twice in the source but gets one node.', an:'Writing “see answer 2” instead of copying the same long working twice in an exam.', tk:'A DAG finds repeated work automatically.',
      n:{say:'Count the nodes: the syntax tree for <code>a + a * (b − c) + (b − c) * d</code> has 13 nodes (7 leaves and 6 operators). The DAG has only 9. Point to the − node with two parents.', ask:'Why is it called acyclic?', ans:'The arrows never form a loop: you can never follow edges from a node and get back to it. Expressions are always like this.', watch:'Sharing is only safe when the values have not changed in between. Inside one expression with no assignments that is always true.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'Building a DAG: value numbering', ic:'#️⃣', p:'Before creating a node, the compiler looks up its <b>signature</b> ⟨op, left child, right child⟩ in a hash table. If it is found, the old node is reused. This is the <b>value-number method</b>.', code:`Building  a + a * (b − c) + (b − c) * d

node 1   a          new
node 2   b          new
node 3   c          new
node 4   ⟨−, 2, 3⟩   new       (b − c)
node 5   ⟨*, 1, 4⟩   new       a * (b − c)
node 6   ⟨+, 1, 5⟩   new
         ⟨−, 2, 3⟩   FOUND → reuse node 4
node 7   d          new
node 8   ⟨*, 4, 7⟩   new       (b − c) * d
node 9   ⟨+, 6, 8⟩   new       the whole expression`, an:'A library catalogue: before buying a book, the librarian checks the catalogue; if the library already has it, no second copy is bought.', tk:'Hash the signature; reuse the node if it already exists.',
      n:{say:'Do this table on the board with the class. The magic moment is the second <code>b − c</code>: its signature ⟨−, 2, 3⟩ is already in the table, so we get node 4 back. The second a also finds node 1.', ask:'Why use a hash table and not just search the list of nodes?', ans:'Searching a list takes longer and longer as the expression grows. A hash table finds a signature in about constant time.', watch:'The children in a signature are node numbers, not names. ⟨−, 2, 3⟩ means “minus applied to node 2 and node 3”.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Three-address code: one small step per line', ic:'3️⃣', p:'In <b>three-address code</b> each instruction has <b>at most one operator</b> on the right-hand side, so it uses at most three addresses. An address is a name (a, x), a constant (2, 3.14) or a <b>temporary</b> (t1, t2…) that the compiler invents to hold an in-between result.', code:`x + y * z      ⟹    t1 = y * z
                     t2 = x + t1`, an:'A long sum done by a careful student: one small step per line, with each partial result written down before it is used.', tk:'Flat, linear and easy to analyse.',
      n:{say:'Ask why it is called three-address: <code>t2 = x + t1</code> mentions three places: t2, x and t1. Then show that the order of the lines replaces the brackets and the precedence rules: y * z must come first.', ask:'Is <code>t1 = a + b * c</code> valid three-address code?', ans:'No. It has two operators on the right. It must be split into <code>t1 = b * c</code> and <code>t2 = a + t1</code>.', watch:'Students reuse a temporary too early, e.g. overwrite t1 while its old value is still needed. Simple rule for now: a new temporary for every operator.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'See it live: x = a + b * c - d;', demo:'livepipe', full:true, input:'x = a + b * c - d;', p:'Press <b>Step</b> until phase 4, <b>Intermediate code</b>. This is real three-address code produced from the syntax tree. Then try your own statement.', an:'Watching a factory line where one station breaks a big parcel into small, labelled boxes.', tk:'t1 = b * c, t2 = a + t1, t3 = t2 - d, x = t3.',
      n:{say:'Stop at phase 2 (tree) and ask the class to predict the TAC before you step to phase 4. The live output is: <code>t1 = b * c</code>, <code>t2 = a + t1</code>, <code>t3 = t2 - d</code>, <code>x = t3</code>. Phase 5 (optimiser) then removes the useless copy: <code>x = t2 - d</code>. Let a student type <code>a = b * -c + b * -c;</code> next.', ask:'Why does b * c come first even though a is written first?', ans:'Because * has higher precedence than +, the tree puts b * c lower down, and code is generated from the bottom of the tree upwards.', watch:'This demo does not insert int → float conversions; keep to int variables here. Conversions were shown in Session 25.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'The kinds of three-address instructions', demo:'tactypes', an:'A small toolbox: a few simple tools (add, copy, jump, call) can build any piece of furniture.', tk:'Assignments, copies, jumps, calls, indexed copies and pointers.',
      n:{say:'Step through each row and give one source example for each. Binary: <code>a + b</code>. Unary: <code>-c</code>. Copy: <code>a = t2</code>. Jumps: from if and while. Calls: <code>f(a)</code> becomes <code>param a</code> then <code>call f, 1</code>. Indexed: <code>arr[i]</code>. Pointers: <code>&amp;v</code> and <code>*p</code>.', ask:'Why do we need both <code>if x goto L</code> and <code>ifFalse x goto L</code>?', ans:'Either one is enough in theory, but having both lets the compiler avoid an extra goto: it jumps only in the case where it must leave the straight-line path.', watch:'<code>call f, 1</code>: the 1 is the number of arguments, not a value passed to f.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Three-address code for a = b * −c + b * −c', demo:'tac', an:'Two friends each walk to the shop to buy the same item, when one trip would have done.', tk:'Six instructions from the tree; a DAG would need only four.',
      n:{say:'After the animation, write the DAG version on the board: <code>t1 = minus c</code>, <code>t2 = b * t1</code>, <code>t3 = t2 + t2</code>, <code>a = t3</code>. Because b * −c is shared, it is computed once.', ask:'Why is it written <code>minus c</code> and not <code>- c</code>?', ans:'To make clear it is the unary minus (one operand), not subtraction (two operands). The word avoids confusion when reading the code.', watch:'Students write t3 = minus c and t1 again. Each new computation gets a new temporary in the naive translation.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'Control flow in three-address code', ic:'🔁', p:'Loops and ifs become <b>jumps</b> to a <b>label</b> (a named position in the code). Arrays become an offset computation and an indexed copy. Here the elements are 8 bytes wide, for example doubles.', code:`do i = i + 1; while (a[i] &lt; v);

L:  t1 = i + 1
    i = t1
    t2 = i * 8          // byte offset = index × width
    t3 = a [ t2 ]       // indexed copy
    if t3 &lt; v goto L    // jump back while the test is true`, an:'A choose-your-own-adventure book: “if you open the door, turn to page 12”.', tk:'Loops become labels and conditional jumps.',
      n:{say:'Trace with i = 0 and an array where a[1] &lt; v but a[2] ≥ v: the body runs, test true, jump to L, body runs again, test false, fall through. A do-while always runs the body at least once, which is why the test is at the bottom.', ask:'Why multiply i by 8?', ans:'Memory is counted in bytes. Element i starts i × 8 bytes after the start of the array when each element takes 8 bytes. Session 27 covers this in detail.', watch:'Forgetting the width: <code>t3 = a[i]</code> would read the wrong bytes for any element width other than 1.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Storing the code: quadruples and triples', demo:'quads', an:'Quadruples are like naming each box (“box t1”); triples are like saying “the box from step 3”.', tk:'Quads name results; triples refer to positions.',
      n:{say:'A compiler must keep three-address code in a data structure. A <b>quadruple</b> has four fields: op, arg1, arg2, result. A <b>triple</b> drops the result field: a later instruction refers to the result by its position, written (0), (1)…', ask:'In the triple <code>(*, b, (0))</code>, what is (0)?', ans:'The result of instruction number 0, i.e. minus c. It is a reference to a position, not the constant zero.', watch:'Students confuse (0) with the number 0. Always write positions in brackets.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'Worked example: quads and triples for x = a + b * c - d', ic:'📋', p:'Start from the three-address code we saw live, then fill both tables. Copy the operator and operands; quadruples keep the temporary names, triples replace each temporary by the position that computed it.', code:`TAC:  t1 = b * c    t2 = a + t1    t3 = t2 - d    x = t3

        quadruples                       triples
 #    op   arg1  arg2  result        op   arg1  arg2
 0    *    b     c     t1            *    b     c
 1    +    a     t1    t2            +    a     (0)
 2    -    t2    d     t3            -    (1)   d
 3    =    t3          x             =    x     (2)`, an:'The same family photo labelled two ways: by name (“Ali”) or by position (“third from the left”).', tk:'Same code, two layouts: names in quads, positions in triples.',
      n:{say:'Fill the tables live, row by row, asking a student for each cell. Point out the last row: in quadruples the copy is (=, t3, —, x); in triples it is (=, x, (2)), following the book’s layout for a copy.', ask:'Which table needs less memory?', ans:'Triples: three fields instead of four, and no temporary names to store.', watch:'This is exactly the short question in Quiz 4. Check students write the operators in the same order as the TAC.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Indirect triples: easy reordering', ic:'🔀', p:'An optimiser often wants to move instructions. In plain triples, moving one changes its position, so every (n) that points to it must be renumbered. <b>Indirect triples</b> keep a separate list of pointers to the triples; the optimiser reorders the list and leaves the triples alone.', code:`instruction list        triples
 35 → (0)          (0)  minus  c
 36 → (1)          (1)  *      b     (0)
 37 → (2)          (2)  minus  c
 38 → (3)          (3)  *      b     (2)
 39 → (4)          (4)  +      (1)   (3)
 40 → (5)          (5)  =      a     (4)`, an:'A playlist: you can reorder the songs in the playlist without moving the song files on your phone.', tk:'Reorder the pointer list, not the triples.',
      n:{say:'Show a move on the board: swap list entries 36 and 37. The triples keep their numbers, so (1) still means b * (0). Nothing else changes.', ask:'Why not just use quadruples, which can also be moved?', ans:'Quadruples are fine and very common. Indirect triples are an option when you want the smaller size of triples and still need to reorder.', watch:'The numbers 35, 36… are just the order of execution; the triple numbers (0), (1)… are the names used inside the triples.'}},
    {k:'Comparison', h:'Choosing a representation', cmp:[['📦 Quadruples',['op, arg1, arg2, result','Temporaries have explicit names','Instructions can be moved freely; optimisers like this']],['🔢 Triples',['op, arg1, arg2; results referred to by position','Less space','Moving an instruction breaks the (n) references']],['🔗 Indirect triples',['Triples + a list of pointers giving the order','Small like triples','Reordering only changes the pointer list']]], an:'Three ways to organise a class list: by name tags, by seat number, or by seat number plus a separate order-of-presentation sheet.', tk:'Quads for easy moving, triples for space, indirect triples for both.',
      n:{say:'Ask the class which one an optimising compiler would choose and why. Accept quadruples or indirect triples with a reason: both allow code motion (moving instructions).', ask:'Which one is used in real compilers today?', ans:'Most modern compilers use quadruple-like instructions with explicit result names, often in SSA form (next slide), stored as objects linked in lists rather than as a table.', watch:'Triples make code motion hard because positions change; that is the classic exam reason.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Static single-assignment form (SSA)', ic:'🧬', p:'In <b>SSA</b> every name is assigned <b>exactly once</b>: each new assignment gets a new version (p1, p2…). Where two paths join, a <b>φ-function</b> picks the version from the path actually taken. <b>LLVM IR</b> and GCC’s <b>GIMPLE</b> use SSA.', code:`p = a + b              p1 = a + b
q = p - c       ⟹     q1 = p1 - c
p = q * d              p2 = q1 * d

if (flag) x = -1;      if (flag) x1 = -1; else x2 = 1;
else      x = 1;       x3 = φ(x1, x2)    // x1 or x2, by the path taken`, an:'Saving each edit of an assignment as a new file: report_v1, report_v2. You always know exactly which version you mean.', tk:'One assignment per name makes many optimisations simpler.',
      n:{say:'Show why it helps: in the original code “p” means two different values at different lines. In SSA, p1 and p2 are clearly different, so an optimiser can see at once which definition each use refers to.', ask:'Does the CPU have a φ instruction?', ans:'No. φ only exists in the IR. Before generating machine code, the compiler replaces it with ordinary copies on each incoming path.', watch:'Keep this slide at the “idea” level; SSA is studied fully in an advanced compilers course.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'IRs in real compilers', ic:'🏭', p:'Real compilers often use several IRs, from high level to low level, each suited to a different job.', pts:[['🐃','<b>GCC:</b> GENERIC trees → GIMPLE (three-address, SSA) → RTL (close to the machine).'],['🐉','<b>LLVM / clang:</b> AST → LLVM IR (three-address, SSA) → machine code.'],['☕','<b>Java:</b> bytecode is a stack-based IR that the JVM runs or JIT-compiles (Session 1).']], an:'A car is designed as a sketch, then a 3-D model, then factory drawings; each form helps a different team.', tk:'Several IRs, each at the right level for its job.',
      n:{say:'Optional live demo: <code>clang -S -emit-llvm hello.c</code> prints LLVM IR; students will recognise three-address instructions such as <code>%3 = mul nsw i32 %1, %2</code>. On godbolt.org pick “LLVM IR” output.', ask:'Is Java bytecode three-address code?', ans:'No. Bytecode is stack-based: <code>iload b, iload c, imul</code> pushes and pops values instead of naming three addresses. It is a different kind of IR.', watch:'Do not let students memorise these names for the exam; the point is that IRs are real and everywhere.'}}
  ],
  lab:{t:'Lab: generate and represent three-address code', steps:['Write three-address code for <code>x = (a + b) * (a + b) - c / d;</code>, first naively, then using a DAG.','Fill the quadruple and triple tables for the naive version.','Write three-address code for <code>while (i &lt; n) { s = s + a[i]; i = i + 1; }</code> (int width 4).','Check the expression answers with <b>Lab tools → Expression → three-address code</b>, or in the live pipeline.'], out:'TAC listings, the two tables and screenshots from the tool.',
    n:{say:'Model answers. Naive: <code>t1 = a + b</code>, <code>t2 = a + b</code>, <code>t3 = t1 * t2</code>, <code>t4 = c / d</code>, <code>t5 = t3 - t4</code>, <code>x = t5</code>. DAG: <code>t1 = a + b</code>, <code>t2 = t1 * t1</code>, <code>t3 = c / d</code>, <code>t4 = t2 - t3</code>, <code>x = t4</code>. Triples (naive): (0) + a b, (1) + a b, (2) * (0) (1), (3) / c d, (4) − (2) (3), (5) = x (4). While loop: <code>L1: if i &lt; n goto L2</code>, <code>goto L3</code>, <code>L2: t1 = i * 4</code>, <code>t2 = a [ t1 ]</code>, <code>t3 = s + t2</code>, <code>s = t3</code>, <code>t4 = i + 1</code>, <code>i = t4</code>, <code>goto L1</code>, <code>L3:</code>.', ask:'The tool shows only quadruples. How do I check my triples?', ans:'Take the quadruple table, delete the result column, and replace every temporary in arg1/arg2 by the number of the row that computed it.', watch:'Students forget the <code>goto L1</code> at the end of the while body, or forget to multiply i by 4.'}},
  disc:['Why do triples make code motion difficult?','Is Java bytecode an IR? What kind?','Why might a compiler use several IRs at different levels?'],
  mistakes:['Putting two operators on one right-hand side.','Reusing a temporary while its value is still needed.','Forgetting to multiply array indices by the element width.','Confusing triple references such as (0) with the constant 0.','Thinking a DAG node can be shared even when a variable changed in between.'],
  rev:['IRs separate front and back ends: m + n parts instead of m × n compilers.','DAGs share common subexpressions; the value-number method finds them with a hash table.','Three-address code: at most one operator on the right; temporaries hold in-between results.','Quadruples have a result field; triples refer to positions; indirect triples add a pointer list for easy reordering.','SSA: each name is assigned once; φ-functions merge values where paths join.'],
  hw:'Read Dragon §6.4. Quiz 4 this week.',
  check:[
    {q:'A DAG for <code>(a + b) * (a + b)</code> has how many <b>operator</b> nodes?', o:['1','2','3','4'], a:1, e:'One + node (shared, because a + b appears twice) and one * node whose two children are both that + node.'},
    {q:'Why are quadruples easier for an optimiser to rearrange than triples?', o:['They are shorter','Results have names, so moving an instruction does not change how others refer to it','They have no operators','They use fewer temporaries'], a:1, e:'Triples refer to results by position; moving an instruction changes its position and breaks those references. Quadruples refer to named temporaries.'},
    {q:'Which line is <b>not</b> valid three-address code?', o:['t1 = minus c','if a &lt; b goto L1','t2 = a + b * c','x = y'], a:2, e:'t2 = a + b * c has two operators on the right-hand side. It must be split into t1 = b * c and t2 = a + t1.'}],
  quiz:[
    {q:'Three-address code allows at most how many operators on the right side?', o:['0','1','2','3'], a:1, e:'One operator.'},
    {q:'In a DAG, a common subexpression is…', o:['Duplicated','Represented by one shared node','Deleted','Turned into a token'], a:1, e:'It becomes one shared node with several parents.'},
    {q:'Which representation has an explicit result field?', o:['Triples','Quadruples','Indirect triples','DFA'], a:1, e:'op, arg1, arg2, result.'},
    {q:'In SSA, each variable is assigned…', o:['Any number of times','Exactly once','Never','Twice'], a:1, e:'Single assignment: every new assignment creates a new version.'},
    {q:'t1 = minus c is a…', o:['Binary operation','Unary operation','Copy','Jump'], a:1, e:'Form x = op y: one operator, one operand.'},
    {q:'With 4 source languages and 5 target machines, how many parts are needed with a shared IR?', o:['20','9','5','4'], a:1, e:'4 front ends + 5 back ends = 9, instead of 4 × 5 = 20 separate compilers.'}],
  assessment:{ kind:'quiz', title:'Quiz 4 — Weeks 11–14', covers:'SDDs, attributes, dependency graphs, SDTs, type checking and scope, implementing L-attributed SDDs, three-address code',
    instructions:['Closed book unless announced.','Show annotated trees and TAC clearly.','Time and marks are announced by the instructor.'],
    mcq:[
      {q:'An L-attributed SDD can be evaluated…', o:['Only after building a cyclic graph','In one depth-first, left-to-right pass','Only bottom-up','Never'], a:1, e:'dfvisit order.'},
      {q:'In D → T L { L.inh = T.type }, information flows…', o:['Up','Sideways from T to L, then down','Only in the lexer','Nowhere'], a:1, e:'Inherited.'},
      {q:'The postfix form of a * (b + c) is…', o:['a b c + *','a b * c +','* a + b c','a b + c *'], a:0, e:'Operands first.'},
      {q:'float x; int i; x = i + 1; requires…', o:['No conversion','Widening the int result to float','A syntax error','Narrowing x'], a:1, e:'Assign int to float → widen.'},
      {q:'Triples refer to results by…', o:['Temporary names','Position of the instruction','Labels','Registers'], a:1, e:'(0), (1), …'}],
    tf:[
      {q:'Every S-attributed SDD is L-attributed.', a:true, e:'No inherited attributes at all.'},
      {q:'Dynamic scope and static scope always give the same result.', a:false, e:'See the f/g example.'},
      {q:'Indirect triples allow instructions to be reordered without renumbering references.', a:true, e:'Only the pointer list changes.'}],
    short:[
      {q:'Write three-address code for x = a + b * c - d.', a:'t1 = b * c; t2 = a + t1; t3 = t2 - d; x = t3.'},
      {q:'Give the quadruples for the same statement.', a:'(*, b, c, t1) (+, a, t1, t2) (−, t2, d, t3) (=, t3, —, x).'}],
    app:[
      {q:'Draw the dependency graph for int a, b and give a topological order.', a:'Nodes: a.entry(1), b.entry(2), T.type(3), L.inh(4), L1.inh(5), addType for b(6), addType for a(7). Edges: 3→4, 4→5, 4→6, 2→6, 5→7, 1→7. Order: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7.'},
      {q:'Type-check and translate: float y; int k; y = k * 2 + 1.5;', a:'k*2: int × int → int: t1 = k * 2. t1 + 1.5: int + float → float: t2 = (float) t1; t3 = t2 + 1.5. Assignment float = float: y = t3.'}],
    note:'Model answers are guides; equivalent correct answers are accepted.' },
  terms:[['Intermediate representation (IR)','A program form between source and target used for analysis and optimisation.'],['DAG','Directed acyclic graph; like a syntax tree but sharing common subexpressions.'],['Value numbering','Hashing node signatures to reuse identical DAG nodes.'],['Three-address code','IR in which each instruction has at most one operator and three addresses.'],['Temporary','A compiler-generated name holding an intermediate value.'],['Quadruple','A TAC record with op, arg1, arg2 and result fields.'],['Triple','A TAC record with op, arg1, arg2; results referenced by position.'],['Indirect triples','A list of pointers to triples, allowing reordering.'],['SSA form','IR in which every variable is assigned exactly once; φ-functions merge values.'],['Retargeting','Moving a compiler to a new machine by writing only a new back end.']]
},
/* ================= WEEK 15 ================= */
{ id:27, week:15, unit:'Intermediate code generation', ic:'➗', lo:[4], book:'Dragon Ch. 6.4',
  title:'Translation of Expressions',
  sub:'An SDD for expressions with addr and code attributes; incremental translation with gen(); assignments and unary minus; array layout, address computation and translation of array references; mixed types.',
  introH:'Teaching the compiler to break sums into steps',
  intro:'Last time we wrote three-address code by hand. Today the compiler does it: a handful of rules attached to the grammar turns <i>any</i> arithmetic expression, assignment or array reference into correct three-address code.',
  introAn:'A recipe book does not list every possible meal. It teaches a few techniques (chop, fry, boil) that combine into thousands of dishes. A few translation rules combine in the same way into code for every expression.',
  introN:{say:'Write three expressions of growing size on the board: <code>x = a</code>, <code>x = a + b</code>, <code>x = (a + b) * c - d / e</code>. Ask: “Do we need a new rule for each one?” The answer of the day: no, one rule per operator is enough, because rules combine through the tree.', ask:'Is this the same as the expression translator we built in the Session 23 lab?', ans:'Very close. There we produced postfix notation; today the output is three-address code, so each operator also gets a temporary.', watch:'Students may expect a long, complicated algorithm. Stress that the whole translator fits on one slide.'},
  obj:['Write an SDD that generates three-address code for expressions using E.addr and E.code.','Use incremental translation (gen) instead of building code strings.','Translate assignments and unary minus.','Compute addresses for one- and two-dimensional array references.','Combine translation with type conversions.'],
  bloom:{head:['<b>Apply</b> the expression SDD.','<b>Compute</b> array addresses.','<b>Trace</b> translations by hand.'],
    heart:['<b>Appreciate</b> how a few rules handle all expressions.','<b>Be accurate</b> with widths and offsets.'],
    hand:['<b>Implement</b> the translation in your parser.','<b>Verify</b> with the Lab tools translator and the live pipeline.']},
  slides:[
    {k:'Concept', h:'The goal: tree in, three-address code out', ic:'🎯', p:'The parser gives us the structure of an expression. We want the compiler to produce the three-address code automatically, for any expression, using a few rules attached to the grammar: an <b>SDD</b> (rules that compute values at each node of the parse tree).', code:`x = ( a + b ) * c ;      ⟹      t1 = a + b
                                  t2 = t1 * c
                                  x = t2`, an:'A cashier’s calculator does not know your shopping list in advance; it just applies + and × to whatever you key in, step by step.', tk:'A few rules, applied at every node, translate every expression.',
      n:{say:'Remind students of the two tools from earlier weeks: the parse tree (Session 10) and SDDs with synthesized attributes (Sessions 20–21). Today we combine them to produce real code.', ask:'Do we translate the parse tree or the syntax tree?', ans:'Either works. The rules are written on grammar productions; the same rules can run during parsing or on a syntax tree afterwards.', watch:'Keep reminding students: one operator → one instruction → one new temporary.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Two attributes: where and how', ic:'🏷️', p:'Every expression node E gets two synthesized attributes, plus two helper functions.', pts:[['📍','<b>E.addr</b>: where the value of E will be: a name, a constant or a temporary.'],['🧾','<b>E.code</b>: the three-address instructions that compute E.'],['🆕','<b>newtemp()</b>: makes a fresh temporary t1, t2… (the book writes <code>new Temp()</code>).'],['✍️','<b>gen()</b>: builds one instruction, e.g. gen(t1 ’=’ a ’+’ b) gives <code>t1 = a + b</code>.']], an:'A parcel at the post office has an address label (where it is going) and a packing list (what is inside and how it was packed).', tk:'addr says where the value is; code says how to compute it.',
      n:{say:'Write on the board: for E = <code>a</code>, addr = a and code is empty. For E = <code>a + b</code>, addr = t1 and code = <code>t1 = a + b</code>. Ask the class for addr and code of <code>(a + b) * c</code> (addr t2; code: two lines).', ask:'Why does a plain name like a have no code?', ans:'Its value is already sitting in variable a. Nothing needs computing, so the address is enough.', watch:'Students mix up addr and code. addr is one name; code is a list of instructions.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'The SDD for expressions and assignments', ic:'📜', p:'Read <code>||</code> as “joined to” (concatenation of code). <code>top.get(id.lexeme)</code> looks the name up in the symbol table of the current scope.', code:`S → id = E ;   S.code = E.code || gen(top.get(id.lexeme) '=' E.addr)

E → E1 + E2    E.addr = newtemp()
               E.code = E1.code || E2.code || gen(E.addr '=' E1.addr '+' E2.addr)
               (E → E1 * E2 is the same with *)

E → - E1       E.addr = newtemp()
               E.code = E1.code || gen(E.addr '=' 'minus' E1.addr)

E → ( E1 )     E.addr = E1.addr ;   E.code = E1.code

E → id         E.addr = top.get(id.lexeme) ;   E.code = ''`, an:'Assembling a report: first the first section, then the second section, then one new summary line at the end.', tk:'Operators make a temporary and one instruction; brackets and names make none.',
      n:{say:'Go rule by rule. For +: first the code of the left operand, then the right, then one new line. For brackets: nothing new, just pass addr and code up. For id: no code at all.', ask:'Why is E1.code placed before E2.code?', ans:'To evaluate left to right, as C and Java require for + here. It also matters when operands have side effects, such as function calls.', watch:'Students create a new temporary for brackets. Brackets only group; they produce no work at run time.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Translating x = ( a + b ) * c', demo:'exprtrans', an:'Building with LEGO: small pieces snap into bigger pieces until the whole model is done.', tk:'Three instructions, two temporaries.',
      n:{say:'Step slowly. At each row ask: which rule is used, what is addr, and is new code produced? Stop at E → ( E1 ) and ask: why nothing new? (Brackets only group.)', ask:'Could the compiler write <code>x = t1 * c</code> directly and skip t2?', ans:'Yes, an optimiser later removes the extra copy. The simple rules always make a temporary, which keeps the translator simple and correct.', watch:'The order of the rows is bottom-up: leaves first, the root last. That is why code for a + b comes before the multiply.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'Worked example: y = -( p + q ) * r', ic:'✍️', p:'Work from the leaves to the root. Write addr and any new code at each node, like filling an <b>annotated parse tree</b>.', code:`node            addr     new code
p, q, r         p, q, r  (none)
p + q           t1       t1 = p + q
( p + q )       t1       (none: brackets only group)
-( p + q )      t2       t2 = minus t1
-( p + q ) * r  t3       t3 = t2 * r
y = …                    y = t3`, an:'Peeling an onion from the inside out: the innermost layer is handled first.', tk:'Unary minus gets its own temporary, written with minus.',
      n:{say:'Draw the tree on the board: * at the root, unary − on the left with ( ) and + below it, r on the right. Annotate each node with addr. Final code: <code>t1 = p + q</code>, <code>t2 = minus t1</code>, <code>t3 = t2 * r</code>, <code>y = t3</code>.', ask:'Why is it not <code>t2 = minus p + q</code>?', ans:'That would be two operators on the right, and it would also mean (−p) + q, which is a different value. The brackets force p + q first.', watch:'Students forget the brackets and apply minus only to p. Check the tree before writing code.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Incremental translation: emit as you go', ic:'⚡', p:'Joining code strings again and again is slow. If <b>gen()</b> writes each instruction straight to one output list, the children’s code is already there in the right order. Then E.code is not needed at all, only E.addr. This is <b>incremental translation</b>.', code:`S → id = E ;   { gen(top.get(id.lexeme) '=' E.addr); }
E → E1 + E2    { E.addr = newtemp(); gen(E.addr '=' E1.addr '+' E2.addr); }
E → - E1       { E.addr = newtemp(); gen(E.addr '=' 'minus' E1.addr); }
E → ( E1 )     { E.addr = E1.addr; }
E → id         { E.addr = top.get(id.lexeme); }`, an:'Writing the final copy of your notes line by line in class, instead of writing rough notes and copying them all again at home.', tk:'A postfix SDT that emits as it goes: only addr is kept.',
      n:{say:'Point out that every action is at the end of its rule, so this is a postfix SDT (Session 23): it runs when the rule is reduced, after both children have already emitted their code. That is why the order still comes out right.', ask:'What would go wrong if gen() ran before the children were translated?', ans:'The instruction would use E1.addr and E2.addr before they exist, and it would appear before the code that computes them.', watch:'This is the same idea as on-the-fly code generation in Session 25, now for expressions.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Check it live: y = -(p + q) * r;', demo:'livepipe', full:true, input:'y = -(p + q) * r;', p:'Press <b>Step</b> to phase 4. Compare the live three-address code with our hand translation. Then type <code>x = a * (b - c) + d;</code> and predict the output before you press Compile.', an:'Checking your long-division answer with a calculator.', tk:'The live compiler agrees: t1 = p + q, t2 = minus t1, t3 = t2 * r, y = t3.',
      n:{say:'Ask a student to predict the code for <code>x = a * (b - c) + d;</code> on the board first. The live output is <code>t1 = b - c</code>, <code>t2 = a * t1</code>, <code>t3 = t2 + d</code>, <code>x = t3</code>. Phase 5 removes the last copy.', ask:'The optimiser wrote <code>y = t2 * r</code>. Is that still correct?', ans:'Yes. t3 was only a stepping stone used once, so writing the result straight into y gives the same value with one instruction less.', watch:'The live demo has no arrays, so use it for plain arithmetic. It does show int → float conversions as <code>(float)</code> lines, e.g. try <code>float z = k * 2 + 1.5;</code>.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'How an array sits in memory', ic:'🧱', p:'Array elements are stored side by side. If the array starts at the <b>base address</b> and each element is w bytes wide, element i is found at an <b>offset</b> (distance in bytes from the start) of i × w.', code:`int A[5];   base = 1000,  w = 4

address:  1000   1004   1008   1012   1016
element:  A[0]   A[1]   A[2]   A[3]   A[4]

address of A[i] = base + i × w        A[3] → 1000 + 3 × 4 = 1012`, an:'A row of lockers, each 4 steps wide. Locker number i is 4 × i steps from the first one.', tk:'1-D: address = base + i × w (indexes start at 0).',
      n:{say:'Draw the row of boxes with addresses under them. Ask for the address of A[0] (the base itself, 1000) and of A[4] (1016). This is why C arrays start at 0: the index is exactly the number of steps from the start.', ask:'What if the array held doubles?', ans:'Then w = 8, and A[3] would be at 1000 + 3 × 8 = 1024.', watch:'The most common error in this topic: forgetting the width and writing base + i.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Translating x = a[i]', demo:'arrayref', an:'To find a seat in a cinema row: count the seats, multiply by the seat width, walk that far from the start.', tk:'An offset computation, then an indexed copy.',
      n:{say:'Step through. The instruction <code>t2 = a [ t1 ]</code> is an <b>indexed copy</b>: “read the value t1 bytes after the start of a”. Writing into an array uses the mirror form <code>a [ t1 ] = x</code>.', ask:'Why is it not <code>t2 = a [ i ]</code>?', ans:'In three-address code the index inside [ ] is a byte offset, not an element number. So the compiler must first multiply i by the width.', watch:'Students think a[t1] means element number t1. In TAC it means the byte at offset t1.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Two-dimensional arrays: row-major order', ic:'🗃️', p:'Memory is one long line, so a table must be flattened. In <b>row-major order</b> the whole of row 0 is stored first, then row 1, and so on. A row of n₂ elements is w₁ = n₂ × w bytes wide.', code:`int A[2][3]   (w = 4, so a row is w1 = 3 × 4 = 12 bytes)

memory:  A[0][0] A[0][1] A[0][2] | A[1][0] A[1][1] A[1][2]
offset:     0       4       8    |   12      16      20

address of A[i][j] = base + i × w1 + j × w      A[1][2] → base + 12 + 8 = base + 20`, an:'Seats in a stadium: to reach row 3, seat 5, first skip three whole rows, then walk five seats along.', tk:'2-D row-major: base + i × (row width) + j × w.',
      n:{say:'Draw the 2 × 3 grid, then “unroll” it into one line of six boxes. Show that A[1][2] is the 6th box, offset 5 × 4 = 20. Same answer as the formula: (1 × 3 + 2) × 4 = 20.', ask:'Where is A[1][0]?', ans:'At offset 1 × 12 + 0 × 4 = 12: the first element of the second row.', watch:'Use n₂, the number of columns, for the row width, not the number of rows. For A[2][3] the row width is 3 elements.'}},
    {k:'Comparison', h:'Row-major vs column-major', cmp:[['➡️ Row-major',['Rows stored one after another','C, C++, NumPy (default), Pascal','A[i][j] at base + (i × n₂ + j) × w','int A[2][3]: A[0][1] at offset 4']],['⬇️ Column-major',['Columns stored one after another','Fortran, MATLAB, R, Julia','A[i][j] at base + (j × n₁ + i) × w','int A[2][3]: A[0][1] at offset 8']]], an:'Reading a class register across each row (row-major) or down each column (column-major).', tk:'Use the layout your language defines; C-family languages are row-major.',
      n:{say:'Work out A[0][1] both ways on the board. Row-major: (0 × 3 + 1) × 4 = 4. Column-major: (1 × 2 + 0) × 4 = 8, because the whole first column (2 elements) comes first. Same element, different place in memory.', ask:'What about Java’s int[2][3]?', ans:'Java has no true 2-D block. It makes an array of 2 references, each pointing to its own int[3] row object. Element access still goes row first, but the rows need not be next to each other in memory.', watch:'Students use the column-major formula for C arrays, or forget that Java uses arrays of arrays.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'Two-dimensional example: c + a[i][j]', ic:'🧮', p:'Each dimension adds “index × width of what that index selects”. For <code>int a[2][3]</code>, index i selects a whole row (12 bytes) and index j selects one int (4 bytes).', code:`int a[2][3];     c + a[i][j]

t1 = i * 12        // i selects a row: 3 ints = 12 bytes
t2 = j * 4         // j selects an int: 4 bytes
t3 = t1 + t2       // total byte offset
t4 = a [ t3 ]      // indexed copy
t5 = c + t4`, an:'A delivery driver: first drive to the right street (row), then walk to the right house number (column).', tk:'Each dimension contributes index × width.',
      n:{say:'Check with i = 1, j = 2: t1 = 12, t2 = 8, t3 = 20, the same offset as on the row-major slide. This is Example 6.12 in the Dragon book.', ask:'Where does the 12 come from? It is not written in the source.', ans:'From the type in the symbol table: a is array(2, array(3, int)). The element type of a is array(3, int), whose width is 3 × 4 = 12.', watch:'Students multiply i by 3 (the number of columns) and forget the 4. The row width is in bytes: 3 × 4 = 12.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'The SDD rules for array references', ic:'📐', p:'A new non-terminal <b>L</b> stands for an array reference. L.addr collects the byte offset; L.type is the type of what the brackets have selected so far; L.array is the array’s symbol-table entry.', code:`L → id [ E ]   { L.array = top.get(id.lexeme);  L.type = L.array.type.elem;
                 L.addr = newtemp();  gen(L.addr '=' E.addr '*' L.type.width); }

L → L1 [ E ]   { L.array = L1.array;  L.type = L1.type.elem;
                 t = newtemp();  L.addr = newtemp();
                 gen(t '=' E.addr '*' L.type.width);
                 gen(L.addr '=' L1.addr '+' t); }

E → L          { E.addr = newtemp();  gen(E.addr '=' L.array.base '[' L.addr ']'); }
S → L = E ;    { gen(L.array.base '[' L.addr ']' '=' E.addr); }`, an:'Following directions: “third building” (one step), then “second floor” (another step added on), then you read the flat number.', tk:'Each [ E ] adds E × (width of the selected part) to the offset.',
      n:{say:'Trace a[i][j]: rule 1 for a[i]: type becomes array(3, int), width 12: <code>t1 = i * 12</code>. Rule 2 for [j]: type becomes int, width 4: <code>t2 = j * 4</code>, <code>t3 = t1 + t2</code>. Rule 3 reads it: <code>t4 = a [ t3 ]</code>. For a store, <code>a[i] = x + 1;</code> gives <code>t1 = i * 4</code>, <code>t2 = x + 1</code>, <code>a [ t1 ] = t2</code>.', ask:'What does <code>.elem</code> mean?', ans:'The element type of an array type: for array(2, array(3, int)) it is array(3, int); for array(3, int) it is int. Each pair of brackets peels off one array level.', watch:'This slide is dense. Keep the focus on the pattern “multiply by the width, then add”, not on memorising the rules.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'Expressions with mixed types', ic:'🔀', p:'Session 25’s rule still applies: find the wider type with max(), <b>widen</b> both operands, then operate. With incremental translation, both operands’ code is emitted first; the conversion comes just before the operation.', code:`float s;  int i;  float a[10];      s = i + a[i];

t1 = i * 4          // a[i]: float is 4 bytes
t2 = a [ t1 ]       // E2 is float
t3 = (float) i      // widen E1: int → float
t4 = t3 + t2
s = t4`, an:'Before adding two bills in different currencies, convert one at the exchange counter, just before the addition.', tk:'Widen first, then operate.',
      n:{say:'Point out the order: E1 is just i (no code). E2, the array element, emits t1 and t2. Only then does the + rule run: max(int, float) = float, widen(i) emits t3, and finally t4. The order follows the rules exactly.', ask:'Why is the conversion not before t1 = i * 4?', ans:'The i inside a[i] is used as an index, which stays an int. Only the i on the left of + must become a float, and the + rule runs after both operands are translated.', watch:'Students convert the index too. Array indexes are always integers; only the operand of + is widened.'}}
  ],
  lab:{t:'Lab: translate expressions and arrays', steps:['Add incremental three-address code generation to your recursive-descent expression parser (each expression function returns its addr).','Translate <code>x = a * (b - c) + d</code> and <code>y = -(p + q) * r</code>. Check both in the live pipeline.','Add 1-D array references with width 4 and translate <code>s = s + a[i * 2]</code>.','For <code>int m[3][5]</code>, translate <code>v = m[i][j] + 1</code> by hand.'], out:'Your code, the three-address code outputs and the hand translation.',
    n:{say:'Model answers: <code>x = a * (b - c) + d</code> → t1 = b - c; t2 = a * t1; t3 = t2 + d; x = t3. <code>y = -(p + q) * r</code> → t1 = p + q; t2 = minus t1; t3 = t2 * r; y = t3. <code>s = s + a[i * 2]</code> → t1 = i * 2; t2 = t1 * 4; t3 = a [ t2 ]; t4 = s + t3; s = t4. <code>v = m[i][j] + 1</code> with row width 5 × 4 = 20 → t1 = i * 20; t2 = j * 4; t3 = t1 + t2; t4 = m [ t3 ]; t5 = t4 + 1; v = t5.', ask:'Do I need a new temporary for <code>i * 2</code> and another for the width?', ans:'Yes: first compute the index (i * 2), then multiply that by the width (4). They are two different operations.', watch:'For m[3][5] many students use 3 instead of 5 for the row width. The row width uses the number of columns.'}},
  disc:['Why does E → ( E1 ) need no new temporary?','Why does C use row-major order and Fortran column-major?','What happens to temporaries in the back end?'],
  mistakes:['Creating a new temporary for parentheses.','Forgetting the element width in array offsets.','Using column-major formulas for C arrays.','Emitting code for E2 before E1 (wrong evaluation order).','Using the number of rows instead of the number of columns for the row width.'],
  rev:['E.addr holds the result; E.code the instructions (or emit incrementally with gen).','Each operator gets a new temporary; parentheses and ids do not.','1-D: base + i × w; 2-D row-major: base + i × w₁ + j × w, where w₁ is the row width.','Each [ E ] adds E × (width of the selected part); then an indexed copy reads or writes the element.','Widen operands before operating on mixed types; incremental translation is a postfix SDT with gen().'],
  hw:'Read Dragon §6.6 (control flow).',
  check:[
    {q:'For <code>int m[3][5]</code> stored row-major, what is the byte offset of <code>m[2][1]</code>?', o:['44','36','20','11'], a:0, e:'A row is 5 × 4 = 20 bytes. Offset = 2 × 20 + 1 × 4 = 44.'},
    {q:'How many instructions does the naive translation of <code>x = (a + b) * (c + d)</code> produce?', o:['3','4','5','6'], a:1, e:'t1 = a + b; t2 = c + d; t3 = t1 * t2; x = t3. Brackets add no instructions.'},
    {q:'In incremental translation, why is E.code no longer needed?', o:['Because expressions have no code','Because gen() writes each instruction straight into one output list, already in the right order','Because the lexer stores the code','Because temporaries are not used'], a:1, e:'Each child emits its instructions before the parent’s action runs, so the output is already in order; only E.addr must be passed up.'}],
  quiz:[
    {q:'For E → ( E1 ), E.addr =', o:['newtemp()','E1.addr','0','id'], a:1, e:'Parentheses only group; no new temporary.'},
    {q:'TAC for x = a + b * c needs how many temporaries (naive)?', o:['1','2','3','0'], a:1, e:'t1 = b * c; t2 = a + t1; x = t2.'},
    {q:'Address of A[5] in an int array (w = 4, base B) is…', o:['B + 5','B + 20','B + 4','B × 5'], a:1, e:'5 × 4 = 20 bytes after the base.'},
    {q:'In row-major int A[2][3], the offset of A[1][1] is…', o:['4','16','12','20'], a:1, e:'1 × 12 + 1 × 4 = 16.'},
    {q:'gen() in incremental translation…', o:['Returns a code string','Appends an instruction to the output','Parses tokens','Allocates registers'], a:1, e:'It emits the instruction directly.'},
    {q:'In column-major int A[2][3], the offset of A[0][1] is…', o:['4','8','12','16'], a:1, e:'Column-major: (j × n₁ + i) × w = (1 × 2 + 0) × 4 = 8.'}],
  terms:[['E.addr','The address (name, constant or temporary) holding the value of E.'],['E.code','The sequence of three-address instructions computing E.'],['gen()','Function that creates/emits one three-address instruction.'],['Incremental translation','Emitting instructions during parsing instead of building code attributes.'],['Row-major order','Array layout where each row is stored contiguously.'],['Column-major order','Array layout where each column is stored contiguously.'],['Element width','Number of bytes occupied by one array element.'],['Indexed copy','The TAC instructions x = a[i] and a[i] = x, where i is a byte offset.']]
},
{ id:28, week:15, unit:'Control flow', ic:'🔀', lo:[4], book:'Dragon Ch. 6.6',
  title:'Control Flow, Boolean Expressions and Short-Circuit Code',
  sub:'Boolean expressions as values vs as jumps; short-circuit (jumping) code for ||, && and !; translating if, if-else and while with labels; the SDD for flow-of-control statements.',
  introH:'Why your program does not crash on a NULL pointer',
  intro:'<code>if (p != NULL &amp;&amp; p-&gt;x &gt; 0)</code> is safe in C only because the second test is <b>skipped</b> when the first one is false. That skipping is called short-circuit evaluation, and the compiler builds it out of jumps.',
  introAn:'A security guard checks your ID card first. If you have no card, he does not bother to check your name on the guest list: the answer is already “no entry”.',
  introN:{say:'Write <code>if (p != NULL &amp;&amp; p-&gt;x &gt; 0)</code> on the board. Ask: “What happens if p is NULL and the computer checks p-&gt;x anyway?” (The program crashes.) Then: “So who makes sure it is not checked?” The compiler, and today we see exactly how.', ask:'Is short-circuiting just a trick to make programs faster?', ans:'No. It is part of the language definition in C, C++, Java, C# and Python. Programs depend on it for safety, so the compiler must do it exactly.', watch:'Students often think the computer evaluates the whole condition and then decides. Today’s key message: conditions are turned into jumps, and some tests are never run.'},
  obj:['Explain the two uses of boolean expressions: computing values and altering control flow.','Generate short-circuit (jumping) code for ||, && and !.','Translate if, if-else and while using labels B.true, B.false and S.next.','Write the SDD for flow-of-control statements.','Explain why short-circuit evaluation is a language rule, not just an optimisation.'],
  bloom:{head:['<b>Explain</b> jumping code.','<b>Apply</b> the control-flow SDD.','<b>Translate</b> nested statements.'],
    heart:['<b>Appreciate</b> how jumps express logic.','<b>Value</b> short-circuit semantics for safe code.'],
    hand:['<b>Write</b> three-address code with labels for nested if/while.','<b>Trace</b> execution paths through the code.']},
  slides:[
    {k:'Comparison', h:'Two uses of boolean expressions', ic:'⚖️', p:'A <b>boolean expression</b> (a condition that is true or false) is used in two quite different ways.', cmp:[['🧮 Computing a value',['flag = a &lt; b &amp;&amp; c','The result is stored, as 1 or 0','Just like arithmetic']],['🔀 Changing the flow of control',['if (a &lt; b &amp;&amp; c) …','The value is never stored','The answer is shown by <b>where the program jumps</b>']]], an:'A cricket umpire can write “OUT” in a notebook (a stored value), or simply raise a finger and the batsman walks off (an action). Inside if and while, conditions work like the raised finger.', tk:'In if and while statements, booleans become jumps.',
      n:{say:'Ask: in <code>if (a &lt; b) x = 1;</code>, does the program ever need to store “true” anywhere? No: it only needs to decide whether to run <code>x = 1</code>. Most of today’s lecture is about this second use.', ask:'What is true in C, as a stored value?', ans:'Any non-zero int counts as true; the comparison operators produce exactly 1 for true and 0 for false.', watch:'Students assume both uses produce the same code. In the jumping form, no 0 or 1 exists anywhere.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'The key idea: a condition is a fork in the road', ic:'🛣️', p:'Every condition B gets two targets, each a <b>label</b> (a named position in the code): <b>B.true</b> (where to go if B is true) and <b>B.false</b> (where to go if false). A statement S also gets <b>S.next</b>: the label of the code right after S. These labels are <b>inherited</b>: they come from the statement around B.', code:`if (a &lt; b) x = 1;            B.true = L1,  B.false = S.next = L2

        if a &lt; b goto L1     // B is true  → jump to L1
        goto L2              // B is false → jump to L2
L1:     x = 1
L2:                          // S.next: whatever comes after`, an:'A fork in the road with two signposts: “true → this way”, “false → that way”. The condition only chooses the road.', tk:'A condition jumps to B.true or to B.false; it never stores a value.',
      n:{say:'Trace the code twice on the board: with a = 1, b = 2 (jump to L1, run x = 1) and with a = 5, b = 2 (fall to goto L2, skip x = 1). Labels are made by <b>newlabel()</b>, which returns a fresh name L1, L2…', ask:'Why are B.true and B.false inherited and not synthesized?', ans:'B cannot know where the then-part or the following statement are; only the if statement around it knows. So the targets are passed down from the parent.', watch:'Students confuse labels with variables. A label is just a name for a position in the code, like a page number.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Short-circuit code for || and &&', ic:'⚡', p:'In <b>short-circuit code</b> (also called <b>jumping code</b>) we stop testing as soon as the answer is known. For <b>B1 || B2</b>: if B1 is true, the whole thing is true, so skip B2. For <b>B1 &amp;&amp; B2</b>: if B1 is false, the whole thing is false, so skip B2.', code:`if (p != NULL &amp;&amp; p-&gt;x &gt; 0)  then-part

      ifFalse p != NULL goto Lfalse   // p is NULL: stop here
      t1 = p-&gt;x                       // only reached when p is not NULL
      ifFalse t1 &gt; 0 goto Lfalse
      ... then-part ...
Lfalse:`, an:'Two locked doors in a row. If the first door is locked, you do not even walk to the second one.', tk:'Test the left side first; test the right side only if it can still change the answer.',
      n:{say:'Read <code>ifFalse x goto L</code> as “if x is false, jump to L”. Show that when p is NULL the line <code>t1 = p-&gt;x</code> is never reached, so nothing crashes. (In real three-address code the field load would be a pointer instruction; we write p-&gt;x to keep it readable.)', ask:'For ||, when is the right side tested?', ans:'Only when the left side is false. If the left side is true, the whole || is already true.', watch:'Students mix the two rules. Memory aid: && stops on false; || stops on true.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Short-circuit code in action', demo:'shortcircuit', an:'A job interview with three questions, where a brilliant answer to the first one gets you hired on the spot.', tk:'No boolean value is ever stored.',
      n:{say:'Step through. Point out that && binds tighter than ||, so the condition means <code>x &lt; 100 || (x &gt; 200 &amp;&amp; x != y)</code>. Try it with x = 50 (first test jumps straight to L2) and with x = 150 (second test sends it to L1).', ask:'With x = 50, how many comparisons run?', ans:'Only one: x &lt; 100 is true, so the whole condition is true and the other two tests are skipped.', watch:'This animation shows the tidy version with ifFalse and no extra gotos. The basic rules (next slides) produce a slightly longer version; Session 29 shows how to tidy it.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'The jumping-code rules', demo:'boolcode', an:'A relay team passing on instructions: each runner tells the next where to go if things go well and where to go if they go badly.', tk:'B.true and B.false are inherited labels, passed down to B1 and B2.',
      n:{say:'Go row by row and draw a small fork diagram for each. For ||: B1 true → whole true (B.true); B1 false → try B2, at a new label. For &&: B1 true → try B2, at a new label; B1 false → whole false (B.false). A comparison makes two instructions: a conditional jump to B.true and a goto to B.false.', ask:'Where is the code for || itself, like an OR instruction?', ans:'There is none. || and && produce no instructions of their own; they only decide which labels B1 and B2 jump to, plus one new label between them.', watch:'For ||, B1.false is a NEW label at the start of B2’s code, not B.false. For &&, B1.true is the new label.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'Translating ! : just swap the labels', ic:'🔁', p:'For <b>B → ! B1</b> no instruction is needed at all. B1 simply jumps to the opposite targets: B1.true = B.false and B1.false = B.true.', code:`if ( !(a &lt; b) ) x = 1;          S.next = L1,  B.true = L2,  B.false = L1
                                 so B1.true = L1, B1.false = L2

        if a &lt; b goto L1        // a &lt; b true  → !(a &lt; b) false → skip
        goto L2
L2:     x = 1
L1:`, an:'Swapping the “Yes” and “No” signs on two doors: the doors stay the same, only the signs change.', tk:'! costs nothing: swap B.true and B.false.',
      n:{say:'Trace with a = 1, b = 2: a &lt; b is true, so we jump to L1 and skip x = 1. Correct, because !(1 &lt; 2) is false. Then with a = 5, b = 2: we reach L2 and run x = 1.', ask:'Is there a NOT instruction anywhere in the output?', ans:'No. The NOT is done entirely by choosing the labels. That is the beauty of jumping code.', watch:'Students swap the labels the wrong way, or also swap them for the parent. Only B1’s targets are swapped.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'Worked example: the rules step by step', ic:'✍️', p:'Here are the basic rules applied to <code>if (x &lt; 100 || x &gt; 200 &amp;&amp; x != y) x = 0;</code>. Each comparison gives a conditional jump plus a goto; || and && only add the new labels L3 and L4.', code:`labels: S.next = L1, B.true = L2   (if),   L3 (||),   L4 (&amp;&amp;)

      if x &lt; 100 goto L2       // B1: true → whole condition true
      goto L3                  // B1: false → try the &amp;&amp; part
L3:   if x &gt; 200 goto L4       // B3: true → test x != y
      goto L1                  // B3: false → whole condition false
L4:   if x != y goto L2
      goto L1
L2:   x = 0                    // the then-part
L1:                            // S.next`, an:'A road map with every junction labelled before the journey starts.', tk:'Basic rules: 3 conditional jumps + 3 gotos; Session 29 removes the useless gotos.',
      n:{say:'Derive the labels on the board. if: B.true = L2 (new), B.false = S.next = L1. ||: B1.true = L2, B1.false = L3 (new), B2.true = L2, B2.false = L1. &&: B3.true = L4 (new), B3.false = L1, B4.true = L2, B4.false = L1. Now each comparison writes its two lines. This is Figure 6.39 in the Dragon book.', ask:'The line <code>goto L3</code> jumps to the very next line. Is it wrong?', ans:'Not wrong, just wasteful. It is exactly what the simple rules produce. Session 29 introduces fall-through, which removes jumps like this.', watch:'Students forget the label L3 and L4 lines. Every new label must also be placed, otherwise the jumps go nowhere.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Translating if-else', demo:'ifelse', an:'A fork in the road where both roads meet again at the next town.', tk:'B.true → S1, B.false → S2, and both continue at S.next.',
      n:{say:'Step through slowly. Stop at <code>goto S.next</code> and ask: what would happen without this line? (After S1 the program would run S2 as well, which is wrong.)', ask:'Why does S2 not need a goto S.next at its end?', ans:'S2 is the last part of the code; the next line after it already is S.next, so control simply falls through.', watch:'The missing <code>goto S.next</code> after S1 is the most common mistake in exam answers.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Translating while', demo:'whiletr', an:'A security guard who checks every visitor: after letting one in, he goes back to the gate and checks the next.', tk:'A label at the top and a goto back to it.',
      n:{say:'Step through. Point out the backward jump <code>goto begin</code>: it is the only thing that makes this a loop. Ask: where do we go when B is false? (S.next: the loop is over.)', ask:'How would do-while differ?', ans:'The body comes first, then the test, and the test jumps back to the start of the body when true. So the body always runs at least once.', watch:'Placing the test after the body turns while into do-while. With while, the test must come first.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'The SDD rules for if and if-else', ic:'📜', p:'These rules say exactly which labels each part gets and in what order the code is joined (<code>||</code> means “joined to”). <code>label(L)</code> places label L at this point in the code.', code:`P → S                    S.next = newlabel()
                         P.code = S.code || label(S.next)

S → if ( B ) S1          B.true = newlabel();  B.false = S1.next = S.next
                         S.code = B.code || label(B.true) || S1.code

S → if ( B ) S1 else S2  B.true = newlabel();  B.false = newlabel()
                         S1.next = S2.next = S.next
                         S.code = B.code || label(B.true) || S1.code
                                  || gen('goto' S.next)
                                  || label(B.false) || S2.code`, an:'A wedding planner’s schedule: first the welcome, then dinner, and a note that says “after dinner, everyone goes to the garden”.', tk:'Labels flow down (inherited); code is joined up (synthesized).',
      n:{say:'Match each rule with the animations you just saw: the if-else S.code line is exactly the if-else picture, top to bottom. P → S makes the very first S.next and places it after all the code.', ask:'Why does the plain if need only one new label?', ans:'If B is false we simply go to whatever follows the if, which is S.next and already exists. Only the start of S1 needs a new label.', watch:'Students write B.false = newlabel() for a plain if. That label would never be placed anywhere; B.false must be S.next.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'The SDD rules for while and for sequences', ic:'🔁', p:'The while rule adds a label at the top and a jump back. A sequence S1 S2 needs a new label between the two statements, because jumps out of S1 must land at the start of S2.', code:`S → while ( B ) S1   begin = newlabel();  B.true = newlabel()
                     B.false = S.next;    S1.next = begin
                     S.code = label(begin) || B.code || label(B.true)
                              || S1.code || gen('goto' begin)

S → S1 S2            S1.next = newlabel();  S2.next = S.next
                     S.code = S1.code || label(S1.next) || S2.code`, an:'A playlist on repeat: at the end of the last song there is a note “go back to song 1”.', tk:'while: label, test, body, goto back. Sequence: a label between S1 and S2.',
      n:{say:'Ask: why is S1.next = begin in the while rule? (After the body, go back to the test.) Why is B.false = S.next? (When the test fails, leave the loop.) Then: in a sequence, why does S1 need its own next label? (If S1 is an if, its false-jump must land at the start of S2.)', ask:'Could the while rule just put goto begin inside S1?', ans:'That is exactly what S1.next = begin achieves: any jump that means “S1 is finished” goes to begin. The final gen(goto begin) covers the case where S1 ends normally.', watch:'Students forget that S1.next = begin, and make the body jump to S.next, which exits the loop after one round.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'Nested example: while with an if inside', ic:'🪆', p:'Each construct adds its own labels; the rules handle nesting automatically. Arrays use the Session 27 rules with int width 4.', code:`while (i &lt; n) { if (a[i] &gt; m) m = a[i];  i = i + 1; }

L1: if i &lt; n goto L2      // while test
    goto Lnext
L2: t1 = i * 4             // if test: a[i] &gt; m
    t2 = a [ t1 ]
    if t2 &gt; m goto L3
    goto L4
L3: t3 = i * 4             // m = a[i]
    t4 = a [ t3 ]
    m = t4
L4: t5 = i + 1             // S1.next of the if = start of i = i + 1
    i = t5
    goto L1                // back to the while test
Lnext:`, an:'Russian dolls: each doll (statement) fits inside a bigger one, and each has its own lid (labels).', tk:'Each construct contributes its own labels.',
      n:{say:'Label the parts: L1 = begin of while, L2 = B.true of while, Lnext = S.next. The body is a sequence (the if, then i = i + 1), so the if gets S.next = L4, a new label placed before <code>i = i + 1</code>. L3 is the if’s B.true. Trace with i = 0, n = 2, a = {5, 9}, m = 0 to show m ends at 9.', ask:'Why is a[i] computed twice (t1 and t3)?', ans:'The simple rules translate each a[i] separately. An optimiser would notice the common subexpression i * 4 and reuse t1.', watch:'Students forget that L4 is needed: when a[i] &gt; m is false, we must skip m = a[i] but still do i = i + 1.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'When a boolean value is really needed', ic:'💾', p:'For <code>flag = a &lt; b || c &lt; d</code> the value must be stored. We still use jumping code for the condition, then jump to code that stores true (1) or false (0).', code:`flag = a &lt; b || c &lt; d

     if a &lt; b goto L1       // true → no need to test c &lt; d
     if c &lt; d goto L1
     flag = false           // both tests failed (C stores 0)
     goto L2
L1:  flag = true            // C stores 1
L2:`, an:'A referee decides by watching the play (jumps), then writes the result on the scoreboard (stores the value).', tk:'When a value is needed, jump to code that stores true or false.',
      n:{say:'Point out that short-circuiting still happens: if a &lt; b, the c &lt; d test never runs. This matters if the right side were a function call such as <code>check()</code>.', ask:'Why not just compute t1 = a &lt; b, t2 = c &lt; d, flag = t1 || t2?', ans:'That would evaluate both sides always, which breaks short-circuit semantics when the right side has side effects or can crash.', watch:'Here the first test jumps straight to L1 and the second follows it with no goto in between; that is the tidy fall-through style. The basic rules would add a goto to a new label after the first test.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Short-circuit is meaning, not optimisation', ic:'🛡️', p:'The language promises that the right side of && or || is <b>not</b> evaluated when the left side decides. Programs rely on this, so the compiler must preserve the skipping exactly.', pts:[['🛡️','<code>p != NULL &amp;&amp; p-&gt;x &gt; 0</code>: without skipping, a <b>null pointer</b> would crash the program.'],['🔢','<code>i &lt; n &amp;&amp; a[i] != key</code>: avoids reading past the end of the array.'],['📞','<code>f() || g()</code>: g() is not even called when f() is true, so its <b>side effects</b> never happen.'],['⚠️','The <b>bitwise operator</b> <code>&amp;</code> on ints evaluates both sides: it is not a short-circuit &&.']], an:'A doctor checks that a patient is not allergic before giving an injection. Skipping that check is not a “speed-up”; the order is the whole point.', tk:'The compiler must keep the skipping exactly as the language defines it.',
      n:{say:'Show a tiny C program: <code>int *p = NULL; if (p != NULL &amp;&amp; *p &gt; 0) printf("yes");</code> runs fine. Change &amp;&amp; to &amp;: with optimisation off it typically crashes with a segmentation fault, because both sides are evaluated.', ask:'In Python, how do <code>and</code> and <code>&amp;</code> differ?', ans:'<code>and</code> short-circuits and works on any values; <code>&amp;</code> is a bitwise operator on integers (or element-wise on NumPy arrays) and always evaluates both sides.', watch:'Students think an optimiser may “reorder the tests to be faster”. It may not change which tests run when they have side effects or can fail.'}}
  ],
  lab:{t:'Lab: write jumping code', steps:['Translate <code>if (a &lt; b &amp;&amp; c &gt; d || e == f) x = 1; else x = 2;</code> using the rules. Remember that && binds tighter than ||.','Translate <code>while (i &lt; n &amp;&amp; a[i] != key) i = i + 1;</code> (int width 4).','For each, mark which tests are skipped when the first test decides the result.','Add if/while three-address code generation to your recursive-descent translator.'], out:'Hand translations with path annotations and your translator’s output.',
    n:{say:'Model answer 1 (S.next = L1, B.true = L2, B.false = L3, new labels L4 for || and L5 for &&): <code>if a &lt; b goto L5</code> · <code>goto L4</code> · <code>L5: if c &gt; d goto L2</code> · <code>goto L4</code> · <code>L4: if e == f goto L2</code> · <code>goto L3</code> · <code>L2: x = 1</code> · <code>goto L1</code> · <code>L3: x = 2</code> · <code>L1:</code>. Model answer 2 (S.next = L1, begin = L2, B.true = L3, && label L4): <code>L2: if i &lt; n goto L4</code> · <code>goto L1</code> · <code>L4: t1 = i * 4</code> · <code>t2 = a [ t1 ]</code> · <code>if t2 != key goto L3</code> · <code>goto L1</code> · <code>L3: t3 = i + 1</code> · <code>i = t3</code> · <code>goto L2</code> · <code>L1:</code>.', ask:'My label numbers are different from the model answer. Is it wrong?', ans:'No. Label names only need to be unique and placed correctly. Check that every jump reaches the right code by tracing a few inputs.', watch:'In answer 1, if a &lt; b is false the c &gt; d test is skipped and control goes to e == f, not straight to the else-part. Many students send it to L3 by mistake.'}},
  disc:['What would go wrong if a compiler evaluated both sides of && always?','How does Python’s "and" differ from "&amp;" on integers?','Why are B.true and B.false inherited, not synthesized?'],
  mistakes:['Evaluating both operands of || before jumping.','Swapping B.true and B.false for ! in the wrong direction, or swapping the parent’s labels too.','Forgetting goto S.next after S1 in if-else.','Placing the while test after the body (that is do-while).','For ||, using B.false as B1.false instead of a new label at the start of B2.'],
  rev:['Booleans in conditions become jumps (short-circuit code); no value is stored.','||: B1 true → B true; &&: B1 false → B false; !: swap the labels, no code.','if-else: B.code, B.true: S1, goto S.next, B.false: S2.','while: begin label, test, body, goto begin; a sequence puts a new label between S1 and S2.','Short-circuiting is required language semantics, not an optional optimisation.'],
  hw:'Read Dragon §6.6.5–6.6.6 (avoiding redundant gotos).',
  check:[
    {q:'For <b>B → B1 || B2</b>, where does B1 jump when it is false?', o:['B.true','B.false','To a new label at the start of B2’s code','S.next'], a:2, e:'If B1 is false, the || might still be true, so we must go on and test B2. B1.false = newlabel(), placed just before B2’s code.'},
    {q:'In <code>if (B) S1 else S2</code>, what happens if the <code>goto S.next</code> after S1 is left out?', o:['Nothing changes','After S1 runs, S2 also runs','S1 never runs','The loop never ends'], a:1, e:'Without the jump, control falls from the end of S1 straight into S2’s code.'},
    {q:'Why must <code>if (x != 0 &amp;&amp; y / x &gt; 2)</code> use short-circuit code?', o:['It is faster','The language says y / x must not be evaluated when x == 0; evaluating it would divide by zero','The parser needs it','It uses fewer temporaries'], a:1, e:'Skipping the right side is part of the meaning of &&. Programs depend on it to avoid crashes such as division by zero.'}],
  quiz:[
    {q:'In B1 || B2, if B1 is true…', o:['B2 is evaluated anyway','B2 is skipped','An error occurs','B is false'], a:1, e:'Short-circuit: the whole || is already true.'},
    {q:'For B → ! B1, the rule is…', o:['B1.true = B.true','B1.true = B.false; B1.false = B.true','gen(not)','B1.true = newlabel()'], a:1, e:'Swap the targets; no code is generated.'},
    {q:'In while (B) S1, after S1 the code jumps to…', o:['S.next','begin (the test)','B.false','Nowhere'], a:1, e:'Loop back to test the condition again.'},
    {q:'B.true and B.false are…', o:['Synthesized','Inherited','Tokens','Temporaries'], a:1, e:'Passed down from the statement around B.'},
    {q:'In if (B) S1 else S2, the instruction after S1.code is…', o:['label B.false','goto S.next','goto B.true','nothing'], a:1, e:'Jump over S2.'},
    {q:'For B → B1 &amp;&amp; B2, B1.false is…', o:['A new label','B.true','B.false','S.next of B2'], a:2, e:'If B1 is false, the whole && is false, so B1 jumps straight to B.false.'}],
  terms:[['Short-circuit code','Code for boolean expressions where operators are implemented with jumps, skipping unnecessary tests.'],['Jumping code','Synonym for short-circuit code.'],['B.true / B.false','Inherited labels to jump to when B is true / false.'],['S.next','Inherited label of the instruction after statement S.'],['newlabel()','Function that creates a fresh label.']]
},
/* ================= WEEK 16 ================= */
{ id:29, week:16, unit:'Control flow', ic:'🧭', lo:[4], book:'Dragon Ch. 6.6.5–6.6.6',
  title:'Control-Flow Translation of Boolean Expressions and Avoiding Redundant Code',
  sub:'The full rules for comparisons and boolean operators; the special fall label and ifFalse; avoiding redundant gotos; boolean values and jumping code together.',
  introH:'Why care about a few extra gotos?',
  intro:'Last lecture our rules turned every condition into jumps, and they worked. But look closely and you will find <code>goto</code>s that jump to the very next line. Today we teach the translator to leave them out, using one small trick: the <b>fall label</b>.',
  introAn:'A tailor who measures twice and cuts once wastes no cloth. A translator that thinks before writing a jump wastes no instructions, and the program runs a little faster every single time.',
  introN:{say:'Write on the board <code>goto L3</code> and on the next line <code>L3: …</code>. Ask: “What does this goto do?” Let students discover that it does nothing useful. Then say: today we stop generating such lines, and we cut the code for a typical condition from 6 jumps to 3.', ask:'Can’t the optimiser just delete these jumps later?', ans:'Yes, a peephole optimiser can (last slide). But it is cheaper and cleaner never to create them. Good translation first, clean-up second.', watch:'Students may think “3 instructions saved” is too small to matter. Remind them that a condition inside a loop runs millions of times.'},
  obj:['Translate comparisons and the operators ||, && and ! with the full basic rule set.','Identify redundant gotos in generated code.','Apply the fall rules using ifFalse.','Translate boolean expressions that are used as values.','Compare instruction counts before and after the fall rules.'],
  bloom:{head:['<b>Apply</b> the fall rules to new conditions.','<b>Analyse</b> generated code for redundant jumps.','<b>Evaluate</b> two translations by counting instructions.'],
    heart:['<b>Value</b> short, clean code.','<b>Take pride</b> in improving code carefully without changing its meaning.'],
    hand:['<b>Rewrite</b> basic jumping code into fall-through code.','<b>Implement</b> the fall rules in a translator.']},
  slides:[
    {k:'Concept', h:'Quick recap: a condition becomes jumps', ic:'🔁', p:'In Session 28, a condition inside <code>if</code> or <code>while</code> became <b>jumping code</b>: it never stores true or false; it jumps. Each condition B gets two exits, each named by a <b>label</b> (a name for a place in the code): <code>B.true</code>, where to go if B is true, and <code>B.false</code>, where to go if B is false.', code:`if (a &lt; b) x = 1;

      if a &lt; b goto L1      // B.true  = L1
      goto Lnext            // B.false = Lnext (after the if)
L1:   x = 1
Lnext:`, an:'A signboard at a road fork: “Lahore this way, Islamabad that way.” The sign does not drive anywhere; it only points to two places.', tk:'A condition = jumps to two labels, B.true and B.false.',
      n:{say:'Draw the four lines on the board. Point at each line: the test, the jump for false, the body, the exit. Remind students that <code>B.true</code> and <code>B.false</code> are inherited attributes: the if statement decides them and hands them down to B. Lnext is <code>S.next</code>, the label of whatever comes after the if.', ask:'Where is the value true or false stored?', ans:'Nowhere. The answer is shown by <i>where the program ends up</i>: at L1 if true, at Lnext if false.', watch:'Some students still expect a line like <code>t1 = a &lt; b</code>. That is the value style, used only when the result must be stored (slide 16).'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'The basic rule for one comparison', ic:'⚖️', p:'A comparison such as <code>a &lt; b</code> uses a <b>relational operator</b> (&lt;, &lt;=, &gt;, &gt;=, ==, !=). The basic rule always makes <b>two</b> jumps: a conditional jump to B.true, then a plain <code>goto</code> to B.false. The constants <code>true</code> and <code>false</code> become one goto each.', code:`B → E1 rel E2    B.code = E1.code || E2.code
                          || gen('if' E1.addr rel.op E2.addr 'goto' B.true)
                          || gen('goto' B.false)
B → true          B.code = gen('goto' B.true)
B → false         B.code = gen('goto' B.false)`, an:'A gatekeeper with two orders: “If you have a ticket, go to Gate 1. Otherwise, go to Gate 2.” Every visitor is sent somewhere.', tk:'Basic scheme: two jump instructions for every comparison.',
      n:{say:'Explain the pieces: <code>E1.code</code> and <code>E2.code</code> compute the two sides (for <code>a[i] &lt; m</code> that is the array code from Session 27). <code>gen()</code> writes one instruction. <code>||</code> here means “glue the code together”, not logical OR. <code>rel.op</code> is the actual operator, e.g. &lt;.', ask:'Why do we need the second goto at all?', ans:'If the test is false the conditional jump is not taken, so the program would run the next line. The goto sends it to B.false instead.', watch:'The symbol <code>||</code> in semantic rules means concatenation of code. Students mix it up with the || operator in the source. Say it out loud every time.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'The basic rules for ||, && and !', ic:'🔗', p:'The operators make <b>no instructions</b> of their own. They only decide which label each part jumps to. <code>newlabel()</code> makes a fresh label name (L1, L2, …). <code>label(L)</code> places that label in the code.', code:`B → B1 || B2   B1.true = B.true;  B1.false = newlabel()
               B2.true = B.true;  B2.false = B.false
               B.code = B1.code || label(B1.false) || B2.code
B → B1 &amp;&amp; B2   B1.true = newlabel();  B1.false = B.false
               B2.true = B.true;  B2.false = B.false
               B.code = B1.code || label(B1.true) || B2.code
B → ! B1       B1.true = B.false;  B1.false = B.true;  B.code = B1.code`, an:'A cricket captain sets the field but does not bowl. ||, && and ! are captains: they tell each comparison where to send the ball.', tk:'||, && and ! only hand out labels; ! simply swaps them.',
      n:{say:'Read the || rule as a sentence: “If B1 is true, the whole thing is true, so B1 jumps to B.true. If B1 is false, we still have hope: go to a new label where B2 starts.” Then do && the same way: “If B1 is false, the whole thing is false. If B1 is true, go on and test B2.” For !, true and false simply change places.', ask:'Why does ! need no instruction?', ans:'Because “not B” is the same as B with its two exits swapped. Swapping two labels costs nothing at run time.', watch:'Common slip: for && students write <code>B1.true = B.true</code>. No: B1 being true does not make the whole && true; B2 must still be tested.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'The basic rules on a real condition', ic:'🧪', p:'Translate <code>if (x &lt; 100 || x &gt; 200 &amp;&amp; x != y) x = 0;</code>. Since &amp;&amp; binds tighter than ||, this means <code>x &lt; 100 || (x &gt; 200 &amp;&amp; x != y)</code>.', code:`labels handed out:   if → B.true = L2 (x = 0),  B.false = L1 (after the if)
  x &lt; 100 : true → L2   false → L3 (new, start of the &amp;&amp;)
  x &gt; 200 : true → L4 (new, start of x != y)   false → L1
  x != y  : true → L2   false → L1

      if x &lt; 100 goto L2
      goto L3
L3:   if x &gt; 200 goto L4
      goto L1
L4:   if x != y goto L2
      goto L1
L2:   x = 0
L1:`, an:'A recipe that says “now go to step 4” when step 4 is the very next line. Correct, but you can feel something is not quite right.', tk:'Basic rules: 6 jump instructions for 3 comparisons.',
      n:{say:'Build this on the board from the top down. First the if gives B.true = L2 and B.false = L1. Then || gives x &lt; 100 its labels, and so on. Write the code only after all labels are known. Count the jumps together: 6.', ask:'Why does x &gt; 200 jump to L1 when false, not to L3?', ans:'If x &gt; 200 is false, the && is false; and since x &lt; 100 was already false, the whole condition is false. So we leave the if: L1.', watch:'Students often forget the precedence of && over ||. Ask them to put the brackets in first.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Spot the waste', demo:'redundant', p:'Look at <code>goto L3</code> followed by <code>L3:</code>. It is a <b>redundant jump</b>: it lands exactly where the program would go anyway. Two more pairs “jump over a jump”: <code>if … goto L4; goto L1; L4:</code> could be one instruction.', an:'Taking a rickshaw to the house next door: you arrive at the right place, but you paid for nothing.', tk:'A goto to the next line, or a jump over a jump, is wasted work.',
      n:{say:'Step through the left column first and ask students to point out each useless jump. Then step to the end and show the right column: same meaning, half the jumps. Do not explain the right column yet; the next slides build it.', ask:'Is <code>goto L1</code> after <code>if x &gt; 200 goto L4</code> also redundant?', ans:'Not on its own: L1 is far away, so this goto is needed in this layout. But the pair “if c goto L4; goto L1; L4:” can be replaced by the single “ifFalse c goto L1”.', watch:'Only one of the three gotos jumps to the very next line. The other two disappear because we flip the test. Be precise here.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'A new instruction: ifFalse', ic:'🔀', p:'<b>ifFalse</b> jumps only when its condition is <b>false</b>. When the condition is true, the program simply continues with the next line. It is one of the standard three-address instructions (Session 26).', pts:[['✅','<code>if a &lt; b goto L</code>: jump when a &lt; b is <b>true</b>.'],['❎','<code>ifFalse a &lt; b goto L</code>: jump when a &lt; b is <b>false</b>.'],['🧮','We never flip the operator by hand: no turning &lt; into &gt;=.']], an:'A teacher says: “If you have NOT finished the homework, go to the library.” Students who finished just stay in their seats.', tk:'ifFalse = jump only when the test fails.',
      n:{say:'Write both forms one under the other with the same condition. Ask: “a = 3, b = 5. Which one jumps?” (the first). “a = 7, b = 5?” (the second). The point: with ifFalse, the <i>true</i> case needs no jump at all.', ask:'Why not just write <code>if a &gt;= b goto L</code>?', ans:'For integers that is the same, but the rule would have to flip every operator correctly. For floating-point values with NaN (“not a number”), “not less than” is not the same as “greater or equal”. ifFalse keeps the rule simple and always right.', watch:'Students read “ifFalse” as “if the value is false, go to L”. Correct, but stress that the condition itself is unchanged.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'The special fall label', ic:'⬇️', p:'The <b>fall label</b> (written <code>fall</code>) is a pretend label. It means “do not jump; continue with the next instruction”, which is called <b>fall-through</b>. We give <code>fall</code> to whichever exit is laid out directly after the code of B.', code:`S → if ( B ) S1     B.true  = fall              // S1 is placed right after B
                    B.false = S1.next = S.next
                    S.code  = B.code || S1.code`, an:'Reading a book: at the bottom of page 12 nobody writes “turn to page 13”. You just turn the page. Only a jump to a far page needs a note.', tk:'fall = “the code you need is right below, no jump”.',
      n:{say:'Compare with the old if rule: B.true used to be <code>newlabel()</code>, and <code>label(B.true)</code> sat between B.code and S1.code. Now B.true is fall, so no label and no jump are needed for the true case.', ask:'Is fall a real label that appears in the output?', ans:'No. It never appears in the code. It is only a signal inside the translator: “no jump needed here”.', watch:'Students sometimes write <code>goto fall</code> in their answers. That instruction must never appear.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'if-else and while also use fall', ic:'🔄', p:'In if-else and while the body S1 is also placed right after the test, so these rules set <code>B.true = fall</code> as well. Only the false exit needs a real label.', code:`S → if ( B ) S1 else S2
      B.true = fall;  B.false = newlabel();  S1.next = S2.next = S.next
      S.code = B.code || S1.code || gen('goto' S.next)
               || label(B.false) || S2.code

S → while ( B ) S1
      begin = newlabel();  B.true = fall;  B.false = S.next;  S1.next = begin
      S.code = label(begin) || B.code || S1.code || gen('goto' begin)`, an:'A school timetable: after the bell you go straight on to the next period in the same room. Only when a class moves to the lab do you need to be told where to go.', tk:'In if, if-else and while: B.true = fall.',
      n:{say:'Point at the two changes compared with Session 28: B.true is fall, and <code>label(B.true)</code> has disappeared from S.code. Everything else is the same, including the <code>goto S.next</code> that jumps over S2, and the <code>goto begin</code> that closes the loop.', ask:'Could we set B.false = fall in if-else instead?', ans:'Only if we placed S2 right after the test, i.e. swapped the two branches. The usual layout puts S1 first, so B.true gets fall.', watch:'Do not remove <code>goto S.next</code> after S1 in if-else. Without it, the program would run S2 after S1.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Comparisons with fall: four cases', ic:'🧩', p:'Now the rule for <code>E1 rel E2</code> checks which exits are fall. Only a real label needs a jump.', code:`test = E1.addr rel.op E2.addr

B.true   B.false    code generated for the test
L1       L2         if test goto L1
                    goto L2
L1       fall       if test goto L1
fall     L2         ifFalse test goto L2
fall     fall       (no code)`, an:'Giving directions to a friend: you mention every turn, but you never say “keep walking straight”.', tk:'With fall, a comparison usually needs one instruction, not two.',
      n:{say:'Go row by row. Row 1 is the old basic rule. Row 2: the false case simply continues. Row 3: the true case continues, so we jump only when false: ifFalse. Row 4: both exits are the next line, so the test is pointless. As before, E1.code and E2.code still come first.', ask:'Which row happens most in practice?', ans:'Row 3 (true = fall, false = a label), because if, if-else and while all set B.true = fall.', watch:'In row 2, students write ifFalse by habit. When B.true is the real label, the jump is a normal <code>if</code>.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'The smallest example: if (a < b) x = 1;', ic:'✂️', p:'With fall, the if gives <code>B.true = fall</code> and <code>B.false = Lnext</code>. That is row 3 of the table, so the test becomes one <code>ifFalse</code>.', cmp:[['Basic rules: 3 instructions',['<code>if a &lt; b goto L1</code>','<code>goto Lnext</code>','<code>L1: x = 1</code>','<code>Lnext:</code>']],['With fall: 2 instructions',['<code>ifFalse a &lt; b goto Lnext</code>','<code>x = 1</code>','<code>Lnext:</code>','(label L1 is no longer needed)']]], an:'Saying “If it is not raining, we play cricket” versus “If it is raining go home; otherwise go to the cricket ground; at the ground, play cricket”. Same plan, fewer words.', tk:'One jump fewer and one label fewer, same meaning.',
      n:{say:'Run both versions by hand for a = 3, b = 5 (x becomes 1) and for a = 9, b = 5 (x unchanged). Both behave the same. Then count: 3 instructions against 2.', ask:'Does the shorter version still short-circuit if the condition were a &lt; b &amp;&amp; c &lt; d?', ans:'Yes. The fall rules change only the layout of the jumps, never the order of the tests or which tests are skipped.', watch:'Make students check both inputs. “Shorter” is only good if the meaning is exactly the same.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'|| with fall', ic:'➕', p:'In B1 || B2, if B1 is false we simply fall into B2: <code>B1.false = fall</code>. If B1 is true we must jump. If B.true is a real label, jump there. If B.true is fall, we still cannot fall, because B2’s code sits in between. So we make a new label and place it <b>after</b> B2.', code:`B → B1 || B2   B1.true  = (B.true ≠ fall) ? B.true : newlabel()
               B1.false = fall
               B2.true  = B.true;   B2.false = B.false
               B.code   = (B.true ≠ fall) ? B1.code || B2.code
                                          : B1.code || B2.code || label(B1.true)`, an:'Two security checks in a queue: if the first guard already lets you in, you walk past the second guard to the door just beyond him.', tk:'||: B1 falls into B2 when false; when true it jumps past B2.',
      n:{say:'Draw three boxes in a column: B1, B2, then-part. Show the arrows: B1 false → down into B2 (fall). B1 true → past B2 to the then-part, so it needs a label just after B2. That label is <code>label(B1.true)</code> at the end of B.code.', ask:'Why is the new label placed after B2 and not before?', ans:'Because the true exit of B1 must skip B2 and reach whatever follows B, which is exactly the spot after B2’s code.', watch:'The most common error with this rule: forgetting <code>label(B1.true)</code> at the end when B.true is fall. Then the jump has nowhere to go.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'&& with fall', ic:'✖️', p:'&& is the mirror image. If B1 is true we fall into B2: <code>B1.true = fall</code>. If B1 is false the whole && is false: jump to B.false, or, if B.false is fall, to a new label placed after B2.', code:`B → B1 &amp;&amp; B2   B1.true  = fall
               B1.false = (B.false ≠ fall) ? B.false : newlabel()
               B2.true  = B.true;   B2.false = B.false
               B.code   = (B.false ≠ fall) ? B1.code || B2.code
                                           : B1.code || B2.code || label(B1.false)`, an:'Getting a visa: if the first document is missing, you are sent home at once; if it is fine, you just move on to the next desk.', tk:'&&: B1 falls into B2 when true; when false it leaves the whole &&.',
      n:{say:'Put the || and && rules side by side and ask students to find the differences: true and false swap roles everywhere. In our usual statements B.true = fall and B.false is a real label, so for && the simple branch is used: no new label at all.', ask:'When would B.false be fall for an &&?', ans:'For example inside !( … &amp;&amp; … ) under an if, because ! swaps the exits: then the && gets true = a label and false = fall, and the newlabel() branch is used.', watch:'Students copy the || rule and forget to swap. Ask: “if B1 is true in an &&, are we done?” (No.)'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'Worked example: handing out labels with fall', ic:'🧾', p:'Back to <code>if (x &lt; 100 || x &gt; 200 &amp;&amp; x != y) x = 0;</code>. Work from the top of the tree down, then read off each comparison’s code from the four-case table.', code:`if         : B.true = fall,  B.false = L1 (S.next)
B1 || B2   : B.true is fall → B1.true = L2 (new, after B2)
             B1.false = fall,  B2.true = fall,  B2.false = L1
x &lt; 100    : true = L2,   false = fall   → if x &lt; 100 goto L2
B3 &amp;&amp; B4   : B.false = L1 is real → B3.true = fall,  B3.false = L1
x &gt; 200    : true = fall, false = L1     → ifFalse x &gt; 200 goto L1
x != y     : true = fall, false = L1     → ifFalse x != y goto L1
then label(L2) from the || rule, then x = 0, then L1:`, an:'A wedding organiser gives each guest a card: “sit here” or “go to Hall 2”. Once every card is written, seating everyone is easy.', tk:'Give out the labels first, then write the code.',
      n:{say:'Do this live on the board as a tree: the if at the root, || below it, && under the right child of ||, and the three comparisons as leaves. Write the two labels next to every node. Then read the leaves from left to right to get the code, and add <code>L2:</code> where the || rule says.', ask:'Why does the && not need a new label?', ans:'Its B.false is L1, a real label, so B3.false = L1 directly. A new label is needed only when the exit we must jump to is fall.', watch:'Students try to write code before labels are fixed and get lost. Insist: labels first, code second.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Result: the compact code', demo:'shortcircuit', p:'The finished code has <b>3 jumps instead of 6</b>, and 4 instructions instead of 7. The tests and their order are exactly the same as before.', an:'The same journey with a better map: fewer turns, same destination.', tk:'Same meaning, half the jumps.',
      n:{say:'Step through the animation and check it against the code from the previous slide line by line. Then trace two inputs: x = 50 (jumps to L2 at once, x becomes 0) and x = 150 (x &lt; 100 is false, so fall to the next line; x &gt; 200 is false, so ifFalse jumps to L1 and x is unchanged).', ask:'Try x = 250, y = 7. What happens?', ans:'x &lt; 100 is false, so we fall to the next line. x &gt; 200 is true, so no jump. x != y is true, so no jump. We reach L2: x = 0.', watch:'Make sure students see that the order of the tests did not change. Changing the order would break short-circuit behaviour.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'fall and ! together: a loop', ic:'🔁', p:'Translate <code>while (a &lt; b &amp;&amp; !(c == d)) a = a + 1;</code>. The while gives true = fall and false = Lnext. Under the !, the exits swap, so <code>c == d</code> gets true = Lnext and false = fall: a normal <code>if</code>, not ifFalse.', code:`L1:     ifFalse a &lt; b goto Lnext     // && : B1 true = fall, false = Lnext
        if c == d goto Lnext         // ! swapped the exits
        a = a + 1
        goto L1                      // back to the test
Lnext:`, an:'A rule in reverse: “Keep playing while there is light and it is NOT raining.” As soon as it rains, you stop; you do not need to check whether it is “not not raining”.', tk:'! with fall can turn an ifFalse into an if, for free.',
      n:{say:'Hand out labels on the board: while → B.true = fall, B.false = Lnext, begin = L1. && → B1.true = fall, B1.false = Lnext, B2.true = fall, B2.false = Lnext. ! → swap: (c == d).true = Lnext, (c == d).false = fall. Row 2 of the table gives “if c == d goto Lnext”. The body is <code>a = a + 1</code> (one TAC instruction; the SDT may write t1 = a + 1; a = t1).', ask:'Why is there no <code>goto Lnext</code> after the loop body?', ans:'After the body we always go back to the test (goto L1). The loop is left only from the two tests.', watch:'Students write <code>ifFalse c != d</code> or flip the operator. The ! never changes the comparison; it only swaps which exit gets the label.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'When the program needs the value', ic:'💾', p:'Sometimes a condition is stored, as in <code>x = a &lt; b &amp;&amp; c &lt; d;</code>. We still use jumping code (so short-circuit still works), with true = fall. Then we add two small assignments: one stores true, one stores false.', code:`        ifFalse a &lt; b goto L1
        ifFalse c &lt; d goto L1
        t = true            // both tests passed
        goto L2
L1:     t = false           // some test failed
L2:     x = t`, an:'An exam result: the examiner goes through the checks one by one; at the end, the clerk writes either “PASS” or “FAIL” on the certificate.', tk:'Value needed: jump first, then write true or false.',
      n:{say:'This is Dragon §6.6.6. The jumps decide, then one of two assignments runs. Trace a = 1, b = 2, c = 5, d = 3: first test passes, second fails, so t = false and x = false.', ask:'Why not compute <code>t1 = a &lt; b; t2 = c &lt; d; t3 = t1 &amp;&amp; t2</code>?', ans:'That always evaluates both sides, which breaks short-circuit rules: with <code>i &lt; n &amp;&amp; a[i] == k</code> it could read outside the array.', watch:'The <code>goto L2</code> after <code>t = true</code> is essential. Without it, t would always end up false.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'The peephole view', ic:'🔍', p:'A <b>peephole optimisation</b> looks at a few neighbouring instructions at a time and replaces wasteful patterns. It could clean up the basic code too, but the fall rules never create the waste in the first place.', pts:[['🗑️','<code>goto L</code> right before <code>L:</code> → delete the goto.'],['↪️','<code>if c goto L1; goto L2; L1:</code> → <code>ifFalse c goto L2; L1:</code>'],['🔗','A jump to a goto → jump straight to the final target.']], an:'Proof-reading an essay through a small window, a few words at a time, to strike out “the the”. Better still is a writer who does not type “the the”.', tk:'Peephole clean-up fixes waste; good translation avoids it.',
      n:{say:'Show that pattern 2 is exactly what turned our 6 jumps into 3. Mention that real compilers do both: they translate carefully and still run a peephole pass, because other phases also create small waste.', ask:'After pattern 2, can we always delete label L1?', ans:'Only if no other jump goes to L1. The translator must check that before deleting a label.', watch:'Students think peephole optimisation changes the program’s logic. It must not: every pattern is a safe local replacement.'}}
  ],
  lab:{t:'Lab: compare basic and fall-through code', steps:['Translate <code>if (a &lt; b || c &lt; d) x = 1; else x = 2;</code> with the <b>basic</b> rules. Count the jump instructions.','Translate the same statement with the <b>fall</b> rules. Count again and compare.','Translate <code>while (i &lt; n &amp;&amp; !(a[i] == key)) i = i + 1;</code> with the fall rules (int width 4).','Translate <code>found = i &lt; n &amp;&amp; a[i] == key;</code>, where the value is needed.','Add the fall rules to your translator and check that its output matches your hand translations.'], out:'Two translations with jump counts, one loop, one value translation, and program output.',
    n:{say:'Answers. Step 1 (basic): <code>if a &lt; b goto L1; goto L4; L4: if c &lt; d goto L1; goto L2; L1: x = 1; goto L3; L2: x = 2; L3:</code> (5 jumps). Step 2 (fall): <code>if a &lt; b goto L1; ifFalse c &lt; d goto L2; L1: x = 1; goto L3; L2: x = 2; L3:</code> (3 jumps). Step 3: <code>L1: ifFalse i &lt; n goto Lnext; t1 = i * 4; t2 = a [ t1 ]; if t2 == key goto Lnext; i = i + 1; goto L1; Lnext:</code>. Step 4: <code>ifFalse i &lt; n goto L1; t1 = i * 4; t2 = a [ t1 ]; ifFalse t2 == key goto L1; t3 = true; goto L2; L1: t3 = false; L2: found = t3</code>.', ask:'In step 3, why must the array code come after the first test?', ans:'Because of short-circuit rules: when i &lt; n is false we must not read a[i] at all; it could be outside the array.', watch:'Label names may differ from the answer key; check the structure (which test jumps where), not the numbers on the labels.'}},
  disc:['Is removing redundant gotos an optimisation or just good translation?','Why does ! need no instructions?','Could the back end remove these gotos instead? What would it need to check?'],
  mistakes:['Setting both exits to fall when they lead to different places: no jump is generated and the test is lost.','Forgetting <code>label(B1.true)</code> after B2 in || when B.true is fall.','Flipping the operator by hand (writing <code>if a &gt;= b</code>) instead of using <code>ifFalse a &lt; b</code>.','Changing the order of the tests, which breaks short-circuit behaviour.','Writing <code>goto fall</code>: fall never appears in the output.'],
  rev:['Basic rules: each comparison gives <code>if … goto B.true</code> plus <code>goto B.false</code>.','||, && and ! create no instructions; they hand out labels (! swaps them).','fall = continue with the next instruction; if, if-else and while set B.true = fall.','With fall, most comparisons need one instruction: <code>if</code> or <code>ifFalse</code>.','When a value is needed: jumping code, then t = true / t = false.'],
  hw:'Read Dragon §6.7 (backpatching) and §6.8 (switch statements).',
  check:[
    {q:'In B1 || B2 with B.true = fall, why does B1.true get a <b>new label</b> instead of fall?', o:['B2’s code sits between B1 and the then-part, so B1 cannot just continue','|| always needs two new labels','fall is only allowed inside &&','B1 is evaluated after B2'], a:0, e:'Falling through from B1 would run B2. The true exit must skip B2, so it jumps to a new label placed after B2.'},
    {q:'Which instruction in the basic code is a goto to the very next line?', o:['<code>goto L3</code> directly before <code>L3:</code>','<code>if x &lt; 100 goto L2</code>','the <code>goto L1</code> that closes a while loop','<code>ifFalse x &gt; 200 goto L1</code>'], a:0, e:'It jumps to where the program would go anyway. The loop’s back jump is needed, and the others are tests.'},
    {q:'Inside <code>!(c == d)</code>, the ! has true = fall and false = Lnext. What does <code>c == d</code> generate?', o:['<code>ifFalse c == d goto Lnext</code>','<code>if c == d goto Lnext</code>','<code>if c != d goto Lnext</code>','nothing'], a:1, e:'! swaps the exits, so c == d gets true = Lnext and false = fall: jump when true, a normal if.'}],
  quiz:[
    {q:'The fall label means…', o:['Jump to the end of the program','Continue with the next instruction (no jump)','An error','A loop'], a:1, e:'fall = fall through to the next instruction.'},
    {q:'If B.true = fall and B.false = L1, a comparison a &lt; b generates…', o:['if a &lt; b goto L1','ifFalse a &lt; b goto L1','goto L1','nothing'], a:1, e:'The true case continues below, so we jump only when the test is false.'},
    {q:'For if (B) S1 with the fall rules, B.true is…', o:['newlabel()','fall','S.next','B.false'], a:1, e:'S1 directly follows the test.'},
    {q:'The basic and fall translations of <code>if (x &lt; 100 || x &gt; 200 &amp;&amp; x != y) x = 0;</code> differ by…', o:['0 instructions','3 unconditional gotos','10 labels','Nothing important'], a:1, e:'6 jumps become 3: the three plain gotos disappear.'},
    {q:'Which part of a boolean expression generates the actual jump instructions?', o:['!','||','&&','The comparisons (E1 rel E2) and the constants true/false'], a:3, e:'||, && and ! only hand out labels (! swaps them); the jumps come from comparisons and constants.'},
    {q:'For B1 && B2 with the fall rules, B1.true is…', o:['B.true','fall','newlabel()','B.false'], a:1, e:'If B1 is true, we simply continue with the code of B2.'},
    {q:'In <code>x = a &lt; b &amp;&amp; c &lt; d</code>, why is there a <code>goto L2</code> after <code>t = true</code>?', o:['To skip <code>t = false</code>','To repeat the test','To call a function','It is redundant'], a:0, e:'Without it, t = false would run next and overwrite the true value.'}],
  terms:[['fall label','A pretend label meaning “continue with the next instruction”; it never appears in the output.'],['ifFalse','Conditional jump taken only when the condition is false.'],['Redundant jump','A jump to the instruction that would execute next anyway.'],['Fall-through','Continuing to the next instruction instead of jumping.'],['newlabel()','Helper that makes a fresh label name.'],['Peephole optimisation','Improving short sequences of neighbouring instructions with local patterns.']]
},
{ id:30, week:16, unit:'Control flow', ic:'🩹', lo:[4], book:'Dragon Ch. 6.7–6.8',
  title:'Backpatching and Switch-Statement Translation',
  sub:'One-pass code generation with lists of jumps: makelist, merge, backpatch; the translation scheme for boolean expressions and statements; translating switch with tests at the end or a jump table.',
  assess:'Assignment-4',
  introH:'Why fill in the blanks later?',
  intro:'When the compiler writes the jump for <code>if (x &lt; 100)</code>, the code it should jump to has not been written yet. <b>Backpatching</b> leaves the target blank, remembers the jump in a list, and fills in the number later, all in one pass. Then we finish the course with the <code>switch</code> statement.',
  introAn:'A wedding card printed before the hall is booked says “Venue: ________”. The family keeps a list of every card sent, and once the hall is confirmed, they phone everyone on the list with the address.',
  introN:{say:'Ask: “You are writing instructions line by line and you reach ‘if the answer is yes, go to line ___’. You have not written that line yet. What do you do?” Students will suggest leaving a gap and coming back. That is backpatching. Tell them this is also the last new topic of the course.', ask:'Why not just read the whole program twice?', ans:'We can (two passes with labels), and many compilers do. But one pass is faster and uses less memory, and it fits bottom-up parsers perfectly.', watch:'Keep the wedding-card picture alive. Later, “list of cards sent” = truelist/falselist/nextlist, and “phoning everyone” = backpatch.'},
  obj:['Explain why backpatching is needed for one-pass translation.','Use makelist, merge and backpatch.','Translate boolean expressions with truelist and falselist.','Translate if, if-else, while and statement lists with nextlist.','Translate switch statements with tests at the end or a jump table.'],
  bloom:{head:['<b>Explain</b> backpatching.','<b>Apply</b> the translation scheme to an expression.','<b>Choose</b> a switch translation strategy.'],
    heart:['<b>Appreciate</b> the cleverness of deciding later.','<b>Value</b> one-pass efficiency.'],
    hand:['<b>Fill</b> quad tables with backpatching by hand.','<b>Implement</b> makelist, merge and backpatch.']},
  slides:[
    {k:'Concept', h:'The problem: jump targets we do not know yet', ic:'❓', p:'Here the compiler writes code in one left-to-right <b>pass</b> into an array of numbered <b>quadruples</b> (100, 101, …). When it writes the jumps for <code>x &lt; 100</code>, the then-part has not been generated yet, so its number is unknown.', code:`if (x &lt; 100) x = 0;

100: if x &lt; 100 goto ___     // where will x = 0 be? not known yet
101: goto ___                 // where does the if end? not known yet`, pts:[['2️⃣','<b>Two passes:</b> write labels now, replace them by numbers in a second pass.'],['🩹','<b>Backpatching:</b> leave the target blank, keep the jump’s number in a list, fill it in as soon as the target is known. One pass.']], an:'Writing “see page __” in your notes while the book is still being printed, and filling in the page numbers when the book arrives.', tk:'Generate now, fill in targets later.',
      n:{say:'Write the two lines on the board with the blanks. Explain that in Session 28 we used labels like L1 and decided their place later; that needs either a second pass or keeping the whole code in memory as text. Backpatching works directly with instruction numbers.', ask:'When do we finally learn the target of quad 100?', ans:'When the parser has seen the condition and reaches the start of the then-part. At that moment nextinstr is the number where x = 0 will go (here 102).', watch:'Students think backpatching means going back and re-reading the source. No: only the blank targets in already generated instructions are filled in.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'nextinstr and the three list functions', demo:'bpfuncs', p:'<b>nextinstr</b> is the number of the next instruction to be generated. A blank target is written <code>_</code>. Three small functions manage lists of unfinished jumps. Example: <code>makelist(101)</code> gives {101}; <code>merge({103}, {105})</code> gives {103, 105}; <code>backpatch({103, 105}, 107)</code> writes 107 into quads 103 and 105.', an:'A class monitor’s list: “students waiting for a room”. Start a list with one name, join two lists together, and when Room 7 is free, send everyone on the list there.', tk:'makelist starts a list, merge joins lists, backpatch fills them all.',
      n:{say:'Step through the animation. Then do the example on the board: draw quads 103 and 105 as <code>goto _</code>, call backpatch with 107, and rub out each _ and write 107. Explain that gen() always writes at nextinstr and then adds 1 to it.', ask:'What does a list actually store?', ans:'Only instruction numbers (indexes into the quad array), not the instructions themselves.', watch:'merge does not change any instruction; it only joins two lists. Only backpatch writes targets.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Lists carried as attributes', ic:'📋', p:'Instead of labels, each part of the tree carries lists of blank jumps. All of them are <b>synthesized</b> attributes, so they are built bottom-up, exactly the way a bottom-up (LR) parser works.', pts:[['✅','<code>B.truelist</code> (the <b>truelist</b>): jumps that must go to the “true” target of B.'],['❌','<code>B.falselist</code> (the <b>falselist</b>): jumps that must go to the “false” target of B.'],['➡️','<code>S.nextlist</code> (the <b>nextlist</b>): jumps that must go to the code right after statement S.']], an:'At a school trip, the teacher keeps three lists: students for the “yes” bus, students for the “no” bus, and students waiting to be told where to go after lunch.', tk:'truelist, falselist, nextlist: lists of jumps still waiting for a target.',
      n:{say:'Compare with Session 28: B.true and B.false were inherited (passed down). Here the lists are synthesized (passed up). That is the whole point: a bottom-up parser can compute them when it reduces, without knowing the targets.', ask:'Why can’t we use inherited labels with an LR parser so easily?', ans:'An LR parser builds the tree from the leaves up. When it reduces x &lt; 100, the parent has not been built yet, so nobody can hand it a label.', watch:'Students mix nextlist (jumps out of a statement) with nextinstr (the next free quad number). Write both on the board and compare.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'The marker M → ε: remembering a position', ic:'📍', p:'A bottom-up parser runs an action only when it reduces a whole production (a postfix SDT). But we must remember <i>where B2 starts</i>, a point in the middle. Trick: put a <b>marker non-terminal</b> M there, with M → ε. When M is reduced, its action saves <code>M.instr = nextinstr</code>.', code:`B → B1 || M B2      // M sits exactly where B2 begins
M → ε               { M.instr = nextinstr; }`, an:'Sticking a coloured bookmark at the start of Chapter 2 while you read. Later you can open the book at exactly that place.', tk:'M matches nothing; it only records the current quad number.',
      n:{say:'Explain timing: the parser reduces B1 first (its code is now generated), then it reduces M → ε, so M.instr is the number of the first instruction of B2. Later, when B → B1 || M B2 is reduced, the action can use M.instr.', ask:'Does M change the language the grammar accepts?', ans:'No. M derives the empty string, so it matches nothing in the input. It only gives the parser a moment to run an action.', watch:'Off-by-one errors: M.instr is the number of the next quad to be written, not the last one written.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Rules for comparisons and constants', ic:'⚖️', p:'A comparison generates its two jumps with blank targets. The conditional jump goes on the truelist, the plain goto on the falselist. Brackets just pass the lists up.', code:`B → E1 rel E2   { B.truelist  = makelist(nextinstr);
                  B.falselist = makelist(nextinstr + 1);
                  gen('if' E1.addr rel.op E2.addr 'goto _');
                  gen('goto _'); }
B → true        { B.truelist  = makelist(nextinstr); gen('goto _'); }
B → false       { B.falselist = makelist(nextinstr); gen('goto _'); }
B → ( B1 )      { B.truelist = B1.truelist; B.falselist = B1.falselist; }`, an:'Two sealed envelopes: one says “open if YES”, one “open if NO”. Both are addressed later.', tk:'One comparison: quad i on the truelist, quad i+1 on the falselist.',
      n:{say:'Do x &lt; 100 at nextinstr = 100: truelist = {100}, falselist = {101}, and quads 100 and 101 are written with blanks. nextinstr is now 102.', ask:'Why makelist(nextinstr + 1) for the falselist?', ans:'Because the goto is the second instruction generated: the if is at nextinstr and the goto at nextinstr + 1.', watch:'The lists are made before gen() is called, while nextinstr still points at the if. Students who call gen() first get numbers that are off by one or two.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Rules for ||, && and !', ic:'🔗', p:'Each rule patches the list it now knows the answer for, and passes the rest up. For ||, if B1 is false we must test B2, which starts at M.instr. For &&, if B1 is true we must test B2. ! just swaps the two lists.', code:`B → B1 || M B2   { backpatch(B1.falselist, M.instr);
                   B.truelist  = merge(B1.truelist, B2.truelist);
                   B.falselist = B2.falselist; }
B → B1 &amp;&amp; M B2   { backpatch(B1.truelist, M.instr);
                   B.truelist  = B2.truelist;
                   B.falselist = merge(B1.falselist, B2.falselist); }
B → ! B1         { B.truelist = B1.falselist;  B.falselist = B1.truelist; }`, an:'A football referee: when one team is clearly decided, he settles them at once; the undecided players wait for the final whistle.', tk:'|| patches B1.falselist; && patches B1.truelist; ! swaps.',
      n:{say:'Read the || rule aloud: “B1 false? Then go to B2: backpatch B1.falselist with the start of B2. B1 true or B2 true means the whole thing is true: merge their truelists. The whole is false only when B2 is false.” Then let students read the && rule themselves.', ask:'Why can’t the || rule patch the truelists too?', ans:'Their target is the then-part of the statement, which is not generated yet. Only the enclosing statement knows it.', watch:'Students patch the wrong list for &&. Ask: “In an &&, when do we need to look at B2?” (When B1 is true.)'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Worked trace: x < 100 || x > 200 && x != y', demo:'backpatch', p:'Quads start at 100. The parser reduces in this order: B1 (x &lt; 100), M (102), B3 (x &gt; 200), M (104), B4 (x != y), then the &&, then the ||. Watch the lists after each step.', an:'Filling a register of answers: each student’s result is written as soon as the teacher knows it.', tk:'Final truelist {100, 104}, falselist {103, 105}.',
      n:{say:'Write the trace on the board as you step. B1: 100 if x &lt; 100 goto _, 101 goto _; T {100}, F {101}. M.instr = 102. B3: 102, 103; T {102}, F {103}. M.instr = 104. B4: 104, 105; T {104}, F {105}. &&: backpatch({102}, 104), so quad 102 gets 104; T {104}, F = merge({103}, {105}) = {103, 105}. ||: backpatch({101}, 102), so quad 101 gets 102; T = merge({100}, {104}) = {100, 104}; F = {103, 105}.', ask:'Why is quad 101 patched with 102, the very next quad?', ans:'If x &lt; 100 is false we must test the rest, which starts at 102. Backpatching with the basic rules can produce such jumps; a peephole pass can remove them later.', watch:'Students forget that the && is reduced before the ||, because && binds tighter. Draw the parse tree first.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Statement rules: if and if-else', ic:'🧱', p:'The statement finally knows the true target: the start of S1, saved by M. For if-else we also need a jump over the else-part. A second marker <code>N → ε</code> generates that <code>goto _</code> and puts it on N.nextlist.', code:`S → if ( B ) M S1
      { backpatch(B.truelist, M.instr);
        S.nextlist = merge(B.falselist, S1.nextlist); }
S → if ( B ) M1 S1 N else M2 S2
      { backpatch(B.truelist, M1.instr);  backpatch(B.falselist, M2.instr);
        S.nextlist = merge(merge(S1.nextlist, N.nextlist), S2.nextlist); }
N → ε { N.nextlist = makelist(nextinstr); gen('goto _'); }`, an:'An exam hall: candidates who pass the check go to Hall A at once. Everyone else, and everyone who finishes, waits outside to be told where the next room is.', tk:'True jumps go to S1 now; everything leaving the statement goes on nextlist.',
      n:{say:'For the plain if: if B is false we leave the statement, and when S1 ends we also leave it. Both kinds of jumps go to “whatever comes next”, which we do not know yet, so they go on S.nextlist. For if-else, N’s goto is the jump from the end of S1 over S2.', ask:'Why is N needed? Why not generate the goto in the final action?', ans:'The final action runs after S2 is generated, too late: the goto must sit between S1 and S2. N is reduced exactly at that point.', watch:'Forgetting to merge S1.nextlist: if S1 is itself an if, its own exit jumps would be lost and stay blank forever.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Statement rules: while and statement lists', ic:'🔁', p:'In a while, M1 marks the test (the loop’s top) and M2 the body. After the body we jump back to the test. In a list of statements, the jumps leaving one statement are patched with the start of the next one.', code:`S → while M1 ( B ) M2 S1
      { backpatch(S1.nextlist, M1.instr);  backpatch(B.truelist, M2.instr);
        S.nextlist = B.falselist;  gen('goto' M1.instr); }
S → { L }       { S.nextlist = L.nextlist; }
S → A ;         { S.nextlist = null; }        // an assignment never jumps
L → L1 M S      { backpatch(L1.nextlist, M.instr);  L.nextlist = S.nextlist; }
L → S           { L.nextlist = S.nextlist; }`, an:'Laps around a cricket ground: at the end of each lap you go back to the start line and check “one more lap?”. Only a “no” lets you leave.', tk:'while: body exits go back to the test; only B.falselist leaves the loop.',
      n:{say:'Point out the only new instruction: <code>goto M1.instr</code>, the loop’s back jump, and it already has its target because M1 was recorded earlier. In L → L1 M S, M marks where the second statement starts, so all exits of the first statement can be filled in.', ask:'Who patches B.falselist of a while?', ans:'Nobody inside the while. It becomes S.nextlist and is patched by the enclosing L → L1 M S when the next statement starts.', watch:'Students put the goto back to the start of the body (M2) instead of the test (M1). The test must run again each time.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'Finishing the example inside an if', ic:'🧾', p:'Put the condition into <code>if (…) x = 0;</code>. M after the bracket records 106, so the truelist {100, 104} is patched with 106. The falselist joins S.nextlist and waits.', code:`if (x &lt; 100 || x &gt; 200 &amp;&amp; x != y) x = 0;

100: if x &lt; 100 goto 106     // truelist, patched by the if
101: goto 102                // patched by the ||
102: if x &gt; 200 goto 104     // patched by the &amp;&amp;
103: goto _                  // on S.nextlist
104: if x != y goto 106      // truelist, patched by the if
105: goto _                  // on S.nextlist
106: x = 0
S.nextlist = merge({103, 105}, { }) = {103, 105}
next statement at 107 (L → L1 M S):  backpatch({103, 105}, 107)`, an:'The last two wedding cards are filled in only when the next event in the family calendar is fixed.', tk:'Every _ becomes a real quad number by the end.',
      n:{say:'Trace x = 50: quad 100 jumps to 106, x = 0. Trace x = 150: 100 no, 101 → 102, x &gt; 200 false → 103 → 107, x unchanged. Trace x = 250, y = 1: 102 → 104 → 106, x = 0. Each trace checks a different path.', ask:'What if the if is the last statement of the program?', ans:'Then {103, 105} is patched with the instruction after the whole program (for example a final halt or return). No jump may be left with a blank target.', watch:'In exam answers, students leave 103 and 105 as “_”. Always state who patches them and with what.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'Worked example: while with if-else inside', ic:'🔄', p:'Translate <code>while (a &lt; b) if (c &lt; d) x = y + z; else x = y - z;</code> from quad 100. The if-else is reduced first (inside), then the while.', code:`100: if a &lt; b goto 102     // while: B.truelist → M2.instr = 102
101: goto _                // while: S.nextlist = B.falselist = {101}
102: if c &lt; d goto 104     // if-else: B.truelist → M1.instr = 104
103: goto 107              // if-else: B.falselist → M2.instr = 107
104: t1 = y + z
105: x = t1
106: goto 100              // N.nextlist {106}; while patches it to M1.instr = 100
107: t2 = y - z
108: x = t2
109: goto 100              // gen('goto' M1.instr) by the while`, an:'A cook who checks the oven, then chooses one of two recipes, then checks the oven again, until the food is done.', tk:'Inner statements are patched first; the loop patches its body’s exits last.',
      n:{say:'Order of events (M1′, M2′ are the inner if-else’s markers): M1.instr = 100; B gives quads 100–101; M2.instr = 102; inner B gives 102–103; M1′ = 104; S1′ gives 104–105; N gives 106 (goto _); M2′ = 107; S2′ gives 107–108. Reduce if-else: backpatch({102}, 104), backpatch({103}, 107), nextlist = {106}. Reduce while: backpatch({106}, 100), backpatch({100}, 102), S.nextlist = {101}, gen 109: goto 100.', ask:'Why does the else-part (107–108) need no goto at its end?', ans:'It falls straight into 109, which is the while’s goto 100. Its nextlist is empty, so nothing needs patching.', watch:'Students number the N goto wrongly or forget it. It sits between S1 and S2, at 106.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Checking a backpatching answer', ic:'✅', p:'Backpatching questions are worth many marks and small slips are common. Before you hand in, run through this list.', pts:[['🌳','Draw the parse tree first: && is reduced before ||, inner statements before outer ones.'],['📍','M.instr is the number of the <i>next</i> quad at that moment, not the last one written.'],['🧮','Each comparison gives two quads: i on the truelist, i + 1 on the falselist.'],['🔗','After every reduction, write the lists. Merged lists keep all their numbers.'],['🩹','At the end, every <code>_</code> is either filled in or on a list, with a note saying who patches it.']], an:'A cricket scorer checks that the runs in the book add up to the scoreboard before signing the scoresheet.', tk:'Tree first, lists after every step, no blank left unexplained.',
      n:{say:'Turn this into a quick class game: show a backpatching answer with one deliberate mistake (for example M.instr = 101 instead of 102) and ask who can find it first.', ask:'Do I lose marks if my quad numbers start at 1 instead of 100?', ans:'Not if the question does not fix the start, but always use the number the question gives. The structure is what matters: which jump goes where.', watch:'Students write the final code without the lists. Examiners usually give marks for the lists at each step, so show them.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Translating switch: tests at the end', demo:'switchtr', p:'A <b>switch statement</b> chooses one of many cases by the value of one expression. The compiler evaluates E <b>once</b> into t, jumps to the tests placed after all the bodies, and ends each body with <code>goto next</code> (that is C’s <code>break</code>).', an:'A receptionist reads your token number once, then checks the list on the wall to find which counter to send you to.', tk:'Evaluate once; bodies first, tests at the end.',
      n:{say:'Why put the tests at the end? The translator reads the cases one by one. For each case it writes the label and body, and remembers the pair (value, label). At the closing brace it knows all the values and writes all the tests together. A code generator can then see the whole group of tests and turn them into a jump table if that is better.', ask:'What happens in C if a case has no break?', ans:'The goto next is simply not generated, so the program falls through into the next case’s body, which is laid out right below. That is C’s fall-through rule.', watch:'Never evaluate E once per case. If E is f(x), the function would be called many times and its side effects would repeat.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'A switch, worked out', ic:'🎛️', p:'Cases 1, 2 and 5 with a default, translated with tests at the end. The book writes the test as <code>if t = 1 goto L1</code>; here we write <code>==</code> to avoid confusion with assignment.', code:`switch (x) {                         t = x               // evaluate once
  case 1: y = 10; break;             goto test
  case 2: y = 20; break;       L1:   y = 10
  case 5: y = 50; break;             goto next           // break
  default: y = 0;              L2:   y = 20
}                                    goto next
                               L3:   y = 50
                                     goto next
                               L4:   y = 0               // default
                                     goto next
                               test: if t == 1 goto L1
                                     if t == 2 goto L2
                                     if t == 5 goto L3
                                     goto L4             // no match
                               next:`, an:'A lift with buttons for floors 1, 2 and 5 and a “lobby” button: you check the buttons in order and go to the lobby if none matches.', tk:'One test per case, then a jump to default.',
      n:{say:'Trace x = 5: goto test, the first two tests fail, the third jumps to L3, y = 50, goto next. Trace x = 3: no test matches, goto L4, y = 0. Point out the four <code>goto next</code> lines: one per break, plus one after default to skip the tests.', ask:'Why is there a goto next after the default body?', ans:'Because the tests come right after it. Without the jump, the program would run into the tests again.', watch:'Students put the default test first. The default must be chosen only after every case value has failed.'}},
    {k:'Comparison', h:'Many cases: jump table or tests?', ic:'⚖️', p:'A <b>jump table</b> is an array of addresses indexed by the switch value. After a range check, one <b>indirect jump</b> (a jump whose target is read from memory) goes straight to the right case.', code:`if t &lt; 1 goto L4          // outside 1..5 → default
if t &gt; 5 goto L4
t2 = t - 1                // index 0..4
goto table[t2]            // table = [L1, L2, L4, L4, L3]  (for 1, 2, 3, 4, 5)`, cmp:[['🔍 Sequence of tests',['Best for a few cases','One test per case, in order','Time grows with the number of cases']],['📇 Jump table',['Best for many <b>dense</b> values (few gaps)','Constant time: one index, one jump','Sparse values: use binary search or a hash table instead']]], an:'Finding a flat: knocking on every door (tests) versus reading the building’s directory board (jump table).', tk:'The compiler picks the strategy from the case values.',
      n:{say:'Check the table entry by entry: value 1 → index 0 → L1, 2 → L2, 3 and 4 → default L4, 5 → L3. The gaps (3 and 4) cost one table entry each. GCC and Clang choose automatically between compare chains, binary search trees of tests and jump tables, depending on how many values there are and how dense they are.', ask:'Why not use a jump table for cases 1, 1000 and 1000000?', ans:'The table would need a million entries, almost all pointing to default. A few tests or a binary search is far better there.', watch:'Forgetting the range check before goto table[t2]: a value outside 1..5 would read outside the table.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Course wrap-up: the whole front end', demo:'finalreview', p:'From characters to three-address code: you have now seen every piece of a compiler’s front end, and built most of them in the labs.', an:'Like finishing a long journey by train: look back at the map and see every station you passed.', tk:'Scanner → parser → recovery → SDD/SDT → types → intermediate code and control flow.',
      n:{say:'Step through the map and, for each box, ask one student to name one thing they can now do (e.g. “build a DFA”, “compute FOLLOW”, “write TAC for a while loop”). Then point them to the Final unit for revision.', ask:'What comes after the front end in a real compiler?', ans:'The back end: optimisation of the intermediate code, instruction selection, register allocation and final machine code. That is usually a second course.', watch:'End on a positive note. Many students found the middle weeks hard; show them how the pieces now fit together.'}}
  ],
  lab:{t:'Lab: backpatching by hand and in code', steps:['Starting at quad 100, translate <code>a &lt; b &amp;&amp; (c &lt; d || e &lt; f)</code> with backpatching. Show truelist and falselist after each reduction.','Translate <code>while (i &lt; n) { if (a &lt; b) x = 1; i = i + 1; }</code> with backpatching, starting at quad 100.','Implement makelist, merge and backpatch using an array of quads and lists of integers.','Translate <code>switch (n) { case 3: r = 1; break; case 4: r = 2; break; case 6: r = 3; break; default: r = 0; }</code> with tests at the end, then with a jump table.'], out:'Two quad tables with lists, working functions, and two switch translations.',
    n:{say:'Answers. Step 1: 100 if a &lt; b goto 102; 101 goto _; 102 if c &lt; d goto _; 103 goto 104; 104 if e &lt; f goto _; 105 goto _. Lists: B1 T{100} F{101}; c &lt; d T{102} F{103}; e &lt; f T{104} F{105}; || backpatches {103} with 104: T{102, 104} F{105}; && backpatches {100} with 102: final T{102, 104}, F{101, 105}. Step 2: 100 if i &lt; n goto 102; 101 goto _ (S.nextlist); 102 if a &lt; b goto 104; 103 goto 105; 104 x = 1; 105 t1 = i + 1; 106 i = t1; 107 goto 100. Step 4 jump table: if t &lt; 3 goto Ld; if t &gt; 6 goto Ld; t2 = t - 3; goto table[t2] with table = [L1, L2, Ld, L3].', ask:'In step 2, who patches quad 103?', ans:'The statement list rule L → L1 M S: the if’s nextlist {103} is patched with M.instr = 105, the start of i = i + 1.', watch:'If a student writes i = i + 1 as one quad, the goto back is at 106, not 107. Both are correct; check the structure, not the exact numbers.'}},
  disc:['Why is backpatching natural for bottom-up parsers?','When is a jump table better than a sequence of tests?','How does C’s fall-through between cases (no break) affect translation?'],
  mistakes:['Patching a list with the wrong M.instr (off by one).','Forgetting to merge S1.nextlist into S.nextlist.','Leaving some jumps unpatched at the end of translation.','Evaluating the switch expression once per case.','Using a jump table without a range check first.'],
  rev:['Backpatching: emit jumps with blank targets, keep lists of them, fill them in later, in one pass.','makelist(i), merge(p1, p2), backpatch(p, i).','B.truelist and B.falselist, S.nextlist; the marker M records nextinstr.','|| backpatches B1.falselist; && backpatches B1.truelist; ! swaps the lists.','switch: evaluate once; tests at the end, or a jump table / binary search for many cases.'],
  hw:'Complete Assignment 4. Start final-exam revision with the Final unit.',
  check:[
    {q:'In <code>B → B1 || M B2</code>, why is B1.falselist backpatched with M.instr?', o:['When B1 is false we must go and test B2, which starts at M.instr','Because B1 false makes the whole || false','Because M.instr is the end of the program','Because truelists are never patched'], a:0, e:'A false B1 does not decide an ||; the code must continue with B2.'},
    {q:'After reducing <code>S → if ( B ) M S1</code>, which jumps are still blank?', o:['None','Those on B.falselist and S1.nextlist, now S.nextlist','Only those on B.truelist','Every jump inside B'], a:1, e:'B.truelist was patched with M.instr. Jumps that leave the statement wait for the next statement.'},
    {q:'A switch has the cases 1, 1000 and 1000000. Which translation fits best?', o:['A jump table indexed by t − 1','A few tests (or a binary search)','Backpatching the cases','A DFA'], a:1, e:'The values are sparse; a jump table would need about a million entries, almost all pointing to default.'}],
  quiz:[
    {q:'backpatch(p, i)…', o:['Creates a list','Inserts i as the target of every jump on list p','Merges lists','Deletes jumps'], a:1, e:'It fills in the blank targets.'},
    {q:'In B → B1 && M B2, which list is backpatched?', o:['B1.falselist','B1.truelist','B2.truelist','None'], a:1, e:'If B1 is true, go and evaluate B2.'},
    {q:'M → ε records…', o:['A token','The number of the next instruction','A type','A scope'], a:1, e:'M.instr = nextinstr.'},
    {q:'For x &lt; 100 || x &gt; 200 &amp;&amp; x != y from quad 100, the final truelist is…', o:['{100}','{100, 104}','{103, 105}','{102}'], a:1, e:'True if x &lt; 100 (quad 100) or if the whole && is true (quad 104).'},
    {q:'A switch with 100 dense cases is best translated with…', o:['100 sequential tests','A jump table','Backtracking','A DFA'], a:1, e:'Constant-time dispatch.'},
    {q:'What does N → ε do in the if-else rule?', o:['Records nextinstr only','Generates the goto _ that jumps over the else-part','Patches B.truelist','Evaluates B'], a:1, e:'N.nextlist = makelist(nextinstr); gen(goto _).'},
    {q:'In S → while M1 ( B ) M2 S1, S1.nextlist is patched with…', o:['M2.instr','M1.instr, the start of the test','B.falselist','Nothing'], a:1, e:'After the body, the loop goes back to the test.'}],
  assessment:{ kind:'assignment', title:'Assignment 4 — Intermediate Code Generation for MiniC', covers:'Weeks 13–16: SDTs, type checking and scope, three-address code, expressions, control flow, short-circuit code, backpatching, switch',
    instructions:['Individual work. Extend your Assignment 2/3 parser.','Show hand translations as well as program output.','Submission date and marks are announced by the instructor.'],
    tasks:['Add type checking with int/float coercions and scope handling (undeclared and redeclared variables).','Generate three-address code for expressions, assignments and 1-D array references (int width 4).','Generate short-circuit code for if, if-else and while using the fall rules.','By hand, translate the program below with backpatching (quads from 100) and show truelist/falselist/nextlist at each step.','Translate a switch statement with at least four cases and a default; explain whether you would use tests or a jump table.','Print the TAC as quadruples and as triples for one function.'],
    scenario:'Program for task 4:<br><code>while (i &lt; n &amp;&amp; a[i] != key) { if (a[i] &gt; max) max = a[i]; i = i + 1; }</code>',
    submission:['PDF with hand translations (tasks 4–5).','Source code + README.','TAC output for at least three test programs.'],
    reflection:['What was the hardest bug in your code generator?','Compare the size of your code with and without the fall rules.'],
    example:`<b>Sample output:</b><pre class="code">100: ifFalse i &lt; n goto 109
101: t1 = i * 4
102: t2 = a [ t1 ]
103: ifFalse t2 != key goto 109
…</pre>`,
    note:'Copied work receives zero.' },
  terms:[['Backpatching','Generating jumps with unknown targets and filling them in later in the same pass.'],['makelist','Creates a list containing one instruction number.'],['merge','Joins two lists of jumps.'],['backpatch','Fills a target into every jump on a list.'],['truelist / falselist','Lists of jumps to patch with the true / false target.'],['nextlist','List of jumps to the code following a statement.'],['nextinstr','The number of the next instruction to be generated.'],['Marker non-terminal','M → ε, used only to record the current instruction number.'],['Switch statement','A statement that chooses one of many cases by the value of one expression.'],['Jump table','An array of addresses indexed by the switch value.'],['Indirect jump','A jump whose target address is read from memory, e.g. from a jump table.']]
},
/* ================= WEEK 17: FINAL ================= */
{ id:99, week:17, exam:true, label:'Final Exam', short:'Final', unit:'Final examination', ic:'🎓', lo:[1,2,3,4],
  title:'Final Exam Preparation',
  sub:'Complete syllabus revision with emphasis on Weeks 10–16: LL(1) implementation, error recovery, SDDs, dependency graphs, SDTs, type checking, three-address code, control flow, backpatching and switch, with a full mock exam.',
  assess:'Final Exam',
  introH:'How to finish the course strong',
  intro:'The final examination covers the <b>whole course</b>, with most weight on Weeks 10–16. This unit gives you a revision path, one practice problem for every big algorithm, and a full mock exam with model answers. The official format, duration and marks are announced by your instructor and the examination office.',
  introAn:'Before a big cricket match a team does not learn new shots. It practises the shots it already knows, again and again, until they come without thinking. Revision is net practice for your brain.',
  introN:{say:'Tell students how this session works: a quick map of the whole course, then one slide per big topic from Weeks 10–16, each with a small problem to solve on paper. Ask them to keep a blank sheet ready; that sheet becomes their one-page summary.', ask:'Which topics will carry the most marks?', ans:'Follow the official paper pattern from the instructor. In general, the algorithm questions (LL(1) traces, dependency graphs, three-address code, backpatching) carry many marks because they need full working.', watch:'Students often revise by reading only. Insist that they solve each mini-problem on paper before you show the answer.'},
  obj:['Revise all phases and algorithms of the front end.','Practise SDD/SDT problems and dependency graphs.','Generate three-address code for expressions and control flow.','Apply backpatching to boolean expressions and statements.','Manage time across theory and algorithm questions.'],
  bloom:{head:['<b>Connect</b> the whole front end into one picture.','<b>Apply</b> every algorithm to new inputs.','<b>Evaluate</b> your own answers against model solutions.'],
    heart:['<b>Approach</b> the exam with confidence and a plan.','<b>Reflect</b> on how far you have come since Week 1.'],
    hand:['<b>Solve</b> a timed full mock exam.','<b>Build</b> a one-page summary sheet.']},
  slides:[
    {k:'Revision', h:'The whole course in one view', demo:'finalreview', p:'Every topic of the course is one station on this line. A program enters as characters and leaves as three-address code.', an:'A train line from Karachi to Peshawar: each station is a topic, and the exam may stop at any of them.', tk:'Scanner → parser → recovery → SDD/SDT → types → intermediate code.',
      n:{say:'Step through the map and, at each box, ask: “What goes in, what comes out?” Scanner: characters in, tokens out. Parser: tokens in, tree out. Recovery: keeps the parser going after an error. SDD/SDT: attributes and translation. Types: checks and conversions. IR: three-address code, control flow, backpatching.', ask:'Which box does the parser hand its tree to?', ans:'To semantic analysis: the SDD/SDT and type-checking boxes attach attributes to the tree and produce intermediate code.', watch:'Students revise topics as islands. Keep asking how each one feeds the next.'}},
    {k:'Revision', h:'A revision plan that works', ic:'🗓️', pts:[['📋','List the topics week by week (next slides) and mark each one 🟢 easy, 🟡 shaky or 🔴 lost.'],['✍️','Spend most time on 🟡: those turn into marks fastest.'],['🔁','For every algorithm, solve one problem from memory, then check it against the lecture.'],['📄','Write mistakes on your one-page summary sheet as you go.'],['⏱️','Do the mock exam under time, then mark it honestly.']], an:'A doctor first checks which part hurts, then treats that part. Do not take medicine for the whole body.', tk:'Revise by solving, not by reading.',
      n:{say:'Give students two minutes to mark the topics on the next slides with colours. Ask a few students to share one 🔴 topic and promise it is covered in this session.', ask:'How many days before the exam should I start?', ans:'At least a week. Spread the topics out and do the mock exam two or three days before, so there is time to fix weak spots.', watch:'Students spend all their time on topics they already like. Gently push them to the 🟡 ones.'}},
    {k:'Revision', h:'Weeks 1–8 in one slide', demo:'midreview', pts:[['🧭','Phases, compiler vs interpreter, the language-processing system.'],['🔤','Tokens, regular expressions, NFA → DFA (subset construction), scanners, Lex.'],['📐','Context-free grammars, ambiguity, left recursion, left factoring.'],['⬇️','Recursive descent, FIRST and FOLLOW, the LL(1) table.']], an:'The foundation of a house: you no longer look at it every day, but everything above stands on it.', tk:'Re-read the Midterm unit for these topics.',
      n:{say:'Do not reteach these. Ask a fast question for each line: “What is a lexeme?”, “What does the subset construction build?”, “How do you remove E → E + T | T left recursion?” (E → T E′, E′ → + T E′ | ε), “Is a left-recursive grammar LL(1)?” (never).', ask:'Will the final ask midterm topics again?', ans:'Yes, the final covers the complete course, but most weight is usually on Weeks 10–16. Check the official pattern.', watch:'Many students forget FIRST/FOLLOW after the midterm, yet LL(1) error recovery (Weeks 10–11) depends on them.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Week 10: the LL(1) parser at work', demo:'ll1trace', p:'The driver keeps a <b>stack</b> that starts as <code>E $</code>. If the top is a terminal, it must <b>match</b> the input. If it is a non-terminal X, look up M[X, a] and replace X by that production’s body, pushed right to left. Stack and input both at $: accept.', an:'A builder with a plan: each step either places the brick that is in hand (match) or looks up the plan to see what to build next (expand).', tk:'Terminal on top: match. Non-terminal: use M[X, a]. Both $: accept.',
      n:{say:'Step through the 17 rows of the trace. Stop at row 5 and ask why T′ → ε was chosen (the input is +, which is in FOLLOW(T′)). Remind students to write three columns in every trace: stack, input, action.', ask:'What happens if M[X, a] is empty?', ans:'That is a syntax error. Without recovery the parser stops; with panic-mode recovery it skips or pops (next slides).', watch:'The top of the stack is on the left in this trace. Students mix up the order when they push the body; the first symbol of the body must end up on top.'}},
    {k:'Revision', h:'Weeks 10–11: error recovery', ic:'🩹', p:'A good parser reports an error clearly, recovers, and keeps going to find more errors. In <b>panic mode</b>, we add synch entries to the LL(1) table: for every a in FOLLOW(A), an empty cell M[A, a] gets “synch”.', pts:[['⬜','Blank cell M[A, a]: <b>skip</b> the input token a.'],['🛑','synch cell: <b>pop</b> A, as if A had been found.'],['❗','Terminal on top that does not match: pop it.'],['🔧','Other strategies: <b>phrase-level recovery</b>, <b>error productions</b>, global correction.']], an:'A tour guide who loses part of the group does not cancel the tour; she skips ahead to the next meeting point and continues from there.', tk:'Blank → skip input; synch → pop A; mismatched terminal → pop.',
      n:{say:'Write the three rules on the board as a table. Then say each other strategy in one sentence: phrase-level fixes things locally (insert a missing ;), error productions add grammar rules for common mistakes, and global correction finds the smallest change but is too costly in practice.', ask:'Why use FOLLOW(A) for synch?', ans:'Tokens in FOLLOW(A) can appear right after an A. If we see one, we can pretend A is finished, pop it, and the parser is back in step with the input.', watch:'Students say “synch → skip the token”. No: synch pops the non-terminal; a blank cell skips the token.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Algorithm check: panic-mode recovery', demo:'panic', p:'Input <code>id * + id</code> has a missing operand. Watch the parser report one error, pop F on a synch entry, and still finish the parse.', an:'A typist who notices a missing word, marks it in red, and keeps typing the rest of the page.', tk:'One error reported, and parsing continues to the end.',
      n:{say:'Stop at the error row: F is on top, input is +. M[F, +] is synch because + is in FOLLOW(F), so F is popped and “missing operand” is reported. Then let the class predict the remaining actions before stepping.', ask:'Why is M[F, +] a synch entry and not a blank cell?', ans:'Because + is in FOLLOW(F) = {*, +, ), $}. A token that can follow F tells us F is missing, so we pop F and carry on instead of throwing the + away.', watch:'Students forget that recovery must not loop forever. Each step must pop the stack or consume input.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Weeks 11–12: S- and L-attributed SDDs', demo:'slattr', p:'A <b>synthesized attribute</b> is computed from a node’s children (flows up). An <b>inherited attribute</b> comes from the parent or siblings. <b>S-attributed</b>: only synthesized. <b>L-attributed</b>: inherited attributes may use only the parent and siblings to the <i>left</i>.', an:'Exam marks are synthesized: they add up from the questions. The class timetable is inherited: it is handed down to you from the department.', tk:'Every S-attributed SDD is L-attributed; not the other way round.',
      n:{say:'Step through the five rules of the animation. For each, ask the class “S? L?” before revealing. The key test for L-attributed: look at every inherited attribute and check that it uses nothing to its right.', ask:'Is <code>A → B C { A.s = B.i }</code> L-attributed?', ans:'Yes. A.s is a synthesized attribute of the head, and those may use any attributes in the body.', watch:'The most common exam slip: calling a rule non-L-attributed because it uses an inherited attribute at all. Inherited is fine if it comes from the left.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Algorithm check: annotated parse tree', demo:'sddtree', p:'For <code>3 * 5 + 4 n</code>, values flow from the digits up to the root. An <b>annotated parse tree</b> shows each node’s attribute values.', an:'A family tree where each person’s savings are the total of their children’s savings: you can only fill it in from the bottom up.', tk:'Synthesized values flow up, in post-order.',
      n:{say:'Ask students to draw the tree themselves before you step. Check: T.val = 15, E.val = 15 + 4 = 19, L prints 19. Then change the input to 2 + 3 * 4 n and ask for the root value (14).', ask:'Why does * get done before +?', ans:'The grammar puts * lower in the tree (T → T * F under E → E + T), so its value is computed first.', watch:'Students write the values but not the rule used. In the exam, write the semantic rule next to each node.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Week 12: dependency graphs and evaluation order', demo:'depgraph', p:'A <b>dependency graph</b> has one node per attribute instance and an edge X → Y when Y needs X. Any <b>topological sort</b> of it is a valid evaluation order. A cycle means no order exists.', an:'Getting dressed: socks before shoes, shirt before tie. Any order that respects these arrows works.', tk:'Edges point from what is needed to what needs it.',
      n:{say:'Use the declaration <code>float id1, id2, id3</code>. Draw the edges: T.type → L.inh → L1.inh → L2.inh, and each inh → its addType. Then let students write two different valid orders.', ask:'Is 4, 5, 6, 1, 7, … a valid order?', ans:'No: node 6 (addType for id3) needs node 3 (id3.entry), which has not been evaluated yet. Every edge must go from earlier to later.', watch:'Arrow direction mistakes are very common. Ask every time: “Which attribute is needed to compute which?”'}},
    {k:'Revision', h:'Week 13: syntax-directed translation schemes', ic:'🔀', p:'An <b>SDT</b> is a grammar with program fragments (actions) inside the bodies. A <b>postfix SDT</b> has every action at the end, so it runs when an LR parser reduces. For an L-attributed SDD, place the actions carefully:', pts:[['⬅️','An action computing an <b>inherited</b> attribute of X goes just <b>before</b> X.'],['➡️','Actions computing <b>synthesized</b> attributes of the head go at the <b>end</b>.'],['🧪','Example: <code>D → T { L.inh = T.type } L</code>']], an:'Stage directions in a play: some are read before an actor enters (“enter smiling”), others at the end of the scene (“lights off”).', tk:'Inherited: just before the symbol. Synthesized: at the end.',
      n:{say:'Write <code>T → F { T′.inh = F.val } T′ { T.val = T′.syn }</code> on the board as a second example and ask where each action sits and why. Link it to the marker M → ε in backpatching: an action in the middle works like a marker.', ask:'Can every SDT be run by an LR parser?', ans:'Postfix SDTs, yes, directly. Actions in the middle need marker non-terminals, which can sometimes create parsing conflicts.', watch:'Students put the inherited action after the symbol. Ask: “Can L use L.inh if it is computed after L?” (No.)'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Weeks 13–14: type checking and scope', demo:'typecheck', pts:[['🔠','<b>Type expressions</b>: integer, array(10, integer), pointer(float), integer → float.'],['🟰','<b>Type equivalence</b>: structural (same shape) or by name.'],['↗️','<b>Coercion</b>: widen int to float, e.g. t1 = (float) i.'],['🏠','<b>Scope</b>: a name refers to the nearest enclosing declaration in the program text.']], an:'At the airport, staff check that each bag matches its label (type checking) and look up the passenger list of your own flight, not another flight (scope).', tk:'Every expression gets a type; mixed types are widened or rejected.',
      n:{say:'Step through <code>x = i * 2.5</code>: i is int, 2.5 is float, so i is widened to float, the product is float, and the assignment is fine. Then ask for the type expression of <code>int a[10][20]</code>: array(10, array(20, integer)).', ask:'What is the type expression of <code>float f(int)</code>?', ans:'integer → float: argument type on the left of the arrow, result type on the right.', watch:'Students widen in the wrong direction (float to int). Coercion goes to the wider type so no information is lost.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'Weeks 14–15: three-address code, quads and triples', ic:'🧾', p:'Practice: <code>x = (a + b) * (a + b) - c</code>. With a <b>DAG</b>, a + b is computed only once. Then store the code as <b>quadruples</b> and as <b>triples</b>.', code:`Three-address code (using the DAG)
t1 = a + b
t2 = t1 * t1
t3 = t2 - c
x  = t3

     Quadruples (op, arg1, arg2, result)     Triples (op, arg1, arg2)
(0)  +   a    b    t1                       +   a    b
(1)  *   t1   t1   t2                       *   (0)  (0)
(2)  -   t2   c    t3                       -   (1)  c
(3)  =   t3        x                        =   x    (2)`, an:'A cook makes the sauce once and uses it in two dishes, instead of cooking the same sauce twice.', tk:'TAC: one operator per line; triples refer to results by position.',
      n:{say:'Let students first write TAC without the DAG (5 instructions: a + b is computed twice), then with the DAG (4). Also revise arrays: <code>x = a[i]</code> with int width 4 is t1 = i * 4; t2 = a [ t1 ]; x = t2. For <code>int a[2][3]</code>, a[i][j] has offset i × 12 + j × 4.', ask:'Why do triples have no result field?', ans:'A triple’s result is named by its own position, e.g. (0). Later instructions refer to (0) instead of a temporary name.', watch:'In triples, the copy x = t3 is written (=, x, (2)): the target name x goes in the first argument.'}},
    {k:'Example', h:'Weeks 15–16: jumping code with fall', ic:'🧭', p:'Practice: <code>if (a &lt; b || c &gt; d) x = 1; else x = 2;</code> with the fall rules. The if-else gives B.true = fall and B.false = L1. For ||, since B.true is fall, a &lt; b gets a new label L2, placed after the second test.', code:`        if a &lt; b goto L2        // || : true exit of B1 must skip B2
        ifFalse c &gt; d goto L1   // B2: true = fall, false = L1
L2:     x = 1
        goto Lnext
L1:     x = 2
Lnext:`, an:'Directions that mention only the turns: “at the first shop turn left, if the gate is closed go round the back”. No “keep walking straight”.', tk:'Labels first, then code; fall means no jump.',
      n:{say:'Hand out labels on the board before writing any code: if-else → B.true = fall, B.false = L1, S.next = Lnext. || with B.true = fall → B1.true = L2 (new), B1.false = fall, B2.true = fall, B2.false = L1. Then read off the code. Count: 3 jumps here versus 5 with the basic rules.', ask:'With a = 5, b = 1, c = 2, d = 7, which assignment runs?', ans:'a &lt; b is false, so fall to the next test; c &gt; d is false, so ifFalse jumps to L1: x = 2.', watch:'Forgetting <code>goto Lnext</code> after x = 1. Without it the else-part runs too.'}},
    {k:'Animation', h:'Algorithm check: backpatching', demo:'backpatch', p:'Practice before you step: translate <code>if (a &lt; b &amp;&amp; c &lt; d) x = 1;</code> from quad 100 and write truelist and falselist after every reduction. Then check the method on the animated example.', an:'Leaving blanks for page numbers in your notes and filling them in once the pages are printed.', tk:'Lists after every step; no _ left unexplained.',
      n:{say:'Answer: a &lt; b gives 100 if a &lt; b goto _, 101 goto _ (T{100}, F{101}); M.instr = 102; c &lt; d gives 102, 103 (T{102}, F{103}); && backpatches {100} with 102, T{102}, F{101, 103}. The if’s M records 104: backpatch({102}, 104); 104 x = 1; S.nextlist = {101, 103}. For switch, revise: evaluate E once, bodies first, tests at the end; many dense cases → jump table.', ask:'Which list does || patch, and which does && patch?', ans:'|| patches B1.falselist (B1 false → test B2); && patches B1.truelist (B1 true → test B2).', watch:'Students forget to say who patches the remaining {101, 103}. Write “patched by the next statement”.'}},
    {k:'Revision', h:'Quick-fire practice', ic:'⚡', p:'Answer each in under a minute, on paper. Answers are in the notes.', pts:[['1️⃣','FOLLOW(T′) for E → T E′, E′ → + T E′ | ε, T → F T′, T′ → * F T′ | ε, F → ( E ) | id?'],['2️⃣','Is <code>A → B C { B.i = C.s }</code> L-attributed?'],['3️⃣','TAC for <code>x = a[i][j]</code>, with <code>int a[2][3]</code>?'],['4️⃣','A switch uses every value from 1 to 50. Tests or jump table?'],['5️⃣','Which phase inserts inttofloat conversions?']], an:'Five quick balls in the nets before the match: short, sharp, and you know at once if your timing is right.', tk:'Quick answers show which topics still need work.',
      n:{say:'Answers: (1) FOLLOW(T′) = FOLLOW(T) = {+, ), $}. (2) No: B’s inherited attribute uses C, a sibling on the right. (3) t1 = i * 12; t2 = j * 4; t3 = t1 + t2; t4 = a [ t3 ]; x = t4 (a row of 3 ints is 12 bytes). (4) A jump table: the values are dense. (5) Semantic analysis (type checking).', ask:'In (1), why is FOLLOW(T′) the same as FOLLOW(T)?', ans:'Because of T → F T′: T′ is at the end of T’s body, so whatever follows T also follows T′.', watch:'In (3), students multiply j by 12 or forget the 4. Row width = number of columns × element width.'}},
    {k:'Concept', h:'Exam-day strategy', ic:'🧭', pts:[['🗺️','Spend the first 5 minutes planning: mark the algorithm questions and give each one a time.'],['✍️','For every algorithm, write the rule you are applying, then the steps.'],['🔍','Check: FOLLOW never contains ε; $ in LL(1) traces; edge directions; array widths; lists merged; no blank _ left.'],['🛌','Sleep the night before. Tired brains make trace errors.']], an:'A batsman looks at the field before facing the first ball, so he knows where the gaps are.', tk:'Clear steps earn marks even if the last line is wrong.',
      n:{say:'Tell students that markers give marks for method. A clean table with one wrong number earns far more than a correct final answer with no working. Suggest answering the question they find easiest first, to settle their nerves.', ask:'What if I run out of time on a long trace?', ans:'Write the method and the first few steps clearly, then say in one line how you would continue. Partial working usually earns partial marks.', watch:'Students spend too long on one question. Remind them to move on when the planned time is up and come back later.'}}
  ],
  lab:{t:'Activity: full mock exam', steps:['Set a timer for the duration announced by your instructor.','Attempt Parts A–D without notes.','Mark your answers using the explanations and model answers.','Write your one-page summary sheet, focusing on the questions you missed.'], out:'Self-marked mock exam + one-page summary.',
    n:{say:'Run the mock exam in class if time allows, or set it as homework under exam conditions. Afterwards, go through the application questions (Part D) together on the board, because they carry the most marks.', ask:'Can my summary sheet include worked examples?', ans:'Yes: one tiny worked example per algorithm is the best kind of summary. Choose the ones you got wrong in the mock.', watch:'Students mark their own answers too kindly. Ask them to compare step by step with the model answer, not just the final line.'}},
  disc:['Which phase of the compiler do you now understand best? Which least?','Explain backpatching to a classmate in two minutes.','Why does a compiler need both an LL/LR parser and an SDD? What would be missing without each?'],
  mistakes:['Revising only definitions and not practising algorithms.','Getting dependency-graph edges the wrong way round.','Forgetting $ in LL(1) traces.','Leaving jump targets as “_” in final answers without saying who patches them.','Calling a rule non-L-attributed just because it uses an inherited attribute from the left.'],
  rev:['Lexical: regular expressions, automata, scanners.','Syntax: CFGs, transformations, recursive descent, LL(1), error recovery.','Semantic: SDDs, SDTs, evaluation orders, type checking, scope.','IR: three-address code, quadruples and triples, expressions, arrays.','Control flow: short-circuit code, fall, backpatching, switch.'],
  hw:'Repeat the mock exam after two days and compare your scores.',
  check:[
    {q:'Which kind of SDD can always be evaluated in one depth-first, left-to-right walk of the tree?', o:['Any SDD whose dependency graph has a cycle','An L-attributed SDD','An SDD whose inherited attributes use right siblings','No SDD can'], a:1, e:'L-attributed SDDs only use information from the parent and from the left, which is already available in a left-to-right walk.'},
    {q:'With the fall rules, why does <code>if (a &lt; b) x = 1;</code> need only one jump?', o:['B.true is fall, so only the false case jumps (ifFalse)','The comparison is deleted','x = 1 is itself a jump','Backpatching removes the jump'], a:0, e:'The then-part is right below the test, so the true case just continues; only the false case needs ifFalse … goto.'},
    {q:'With backpatching, <code>a &lt; b &amp;&amp; c &lt; d</code> is translated from quad 100. What is its falselist?', o:['{100, 102}','{101, 103}','{102}','{103}'], a:1, e:'Both plain gotos (101 and 103) lead to “false”; && merges B1.falselist and B2.falselist.'}],
  quiz:[
    {q:'Which is an L-attributed but not S-attributed rule?', o:['E.val = E1.val + T.val','L.inh = T.type in D → T L','F.val = digit.lexval','T.val = F.val'], a:1, e:'An inherited attribute taken from a left sibling.'},
    {q:'In short-circuit code for B1 &amp;&amp; B2, if B1 is false we jump to…', o:['B2','B.false','B.true','S.next always'], a:1, e:'The whole && is false.'},
    {q:'makelist(i) returns…', o:['An empty list','A list containing only i','All quads','A label'], a:1, e:'A one-element list.'},
    {q:'The type expression of float f(int) is…', o:['integer → float','float → integer','array(int, float)','pointer(float)'], a:0, e:'Argument → result.'},
    {q:'For x = a[i] with int width 4, the first instruction is…', o:['x = a','t1 = i * 4','t1 = a + i','goto L'], a:1, e:'Compute the byte offset first.'},
    {q:'In panic mode, the parser has A on top and M[A, a] is synch. It…', o:['Skips a','Pops A','Stops','Pushes A again'], a:1, e:'synch → pop A; a blank cell would skip a.'},
    {q:'FOLLOW(T′) for the usual expression grammar (E → T E′, T → F T′, T′ → * F T′ | ε, …) is…', o:['{*, $}','{+, ), $}','{id, (}','{ε}'], a:1, e:'T′ ends T’s body, so FOLLOW(T′) = FOLLOW(T) = {+, ), $}.'}],
  assessment:{ kind:'exam', title:'Final Mock Examination (Complete Course)', covers:'All topics, Weeks 1–16',
    instructions:['Attempt all questions.','Show working for every algorithm.','Official duration and marks are announced by the instructor.'],
    mcq:[
      {q:'Which phase inserts inttofloat conversions?', o:['Lexical','Syntax','Semantic','Linking'], a:2, e:'Type checking.'},
      {q:'The subset construction converts…', o:['RE → NFA','NFA → DFA','CFG → LL(1)','SDD → SDT'], a:1, e:'NFA to DFA.'},
      {q:'A grammar with left recursion is…', o:['Always LL(1)','Never LL(1)','Always ambiguous','Regular'], a:1, e:'Conflict in the table.'},
      {q:'synch entries are added for terminals in…', o:['FIRST(A)','FOLLOW(A)','The symbol table','Every cell'], a:1, e:'FOLLOW sets (empty cells).'},
      {q:'A dependency graph with a cycle means…', o:['Any order works','No evaluation order exists','The SDD is S-attributed','A syntax error'], a:1, e:'Circular.'},
      {q:'Which form has no result field?', o:['Quadruples','Triples','SSA','Assembly'], a:1, e:'Results by position.'},
      {q:'With the fall rules, if (a < b) x = 1; generates the test…', o:['if a < b goto L','ifFalse a < b goto S.next','goto S.next','nothing'], a:1, e:'Jump only when false.'},
      {q:'In B → B1 || M B2, backpatch is called on…', o:['B1.truelist','B1.falselist','B2.falselist','M.instr'], a:1, e:'If B1 is false, evaluate B2.'}],
    tf:[
      {q:'Static scope resolves names by the program text.', a:true, e:'Lexical scoping.'},
      {q:'Every L-attributed SDD is S-attributed.', a:false, e:'The reverse is true.'},
      {q:'Short-circuit evaluation is only an optimisation and can be dropped.', a:false, e:'It is part of the language semantics.'},
      {q:'A DAG can reduce the number of three-address instructions.', a:true, e:'Common subexpressions are computed once.'},
      {q:'Backpatching requires two passes over the program.', a:false, e:'Its purpose is one-pass translation.'}],
    short:[
      {q:'Define S-attributed and L-attributed SDDs.', a:'S-attributed: all attributes synthesized. L-attributed: each inherited attribute of Xᵢ in A → X₁…Xₙ depends only on inherited attributes of A, attributes of X₁…Xᵢ₋₁, and attributes of Xᵢ itself without cycles; synthesized attributes are allowed.'},
      {q:'Write three-address code for a = b * -c + b * -c and its triples.', a:'t1 = minus c; t2 = b * t1; t3 = minus c; t4 = b * t3; t5 = t2 + t4; a = t5. Triples: (0) minus c; (1) * b (0); (2) minus c; (3) * b (2); (4) + (1) (3); (5) = a (4).'},
      {q:'State the three panic-mode recovery rules of an LL(1) parser.', a:'Blank M[A,a]: skip a. synch M[A,a]: pop A. Terminal on top not matching: pop it.'}],
    app:[
      {q:'Translate with backpatching (quads from 100): if (a < b || c < d && e < f) x = 1;', a:'100: if a < b goto 106; 101: goto 102; 102: if c < d goto 104; 103: goto next; 104: if e < f goto 106; 105: goto next; 106: x = 1. B1: T{100} F{101}; B3: T{102} F{103}; B4: T{104} F{105}; B3&&B4: backpatch({102},104), T{104} F{103,105}; B1||…: backpatch({101},102), T{100,104} F{103,105}; if: backpatch(T,106), nextlist {103,105}.'},
      {q:'Give an SDD with inherited attributes for int a, b, c and draw its dependency graph order.', a:'D → T L {L.inh = T.type}; T → int {T.type = integer}; L → L1 , id {L1.inh = L.inh; addType(id.entry, L.inh)}; L → id {addType(id.entry, L.inh)}. Order: id entries, T.type, L.inh, addType(c), L1.inh, addType(b), L2.inh, addType(a).'},
      {q:'Generate short-circuit code with fall for: while (i < n && a[i] != 0) i = i + 1; (int width 4).', a:'L1: ifFalse i < n goto Lnext; t1 = i * 4; t2 = a [ t1 ]; ifFalse t2 != 0 goto Lnext; i = i + 1; goto L1; Lnext:'}],
    note:'Model answers are guides; equivalent correct answers are accepted.' },
  terms:[['Final examination','The Week 17 examination covering the complete course.'],['Mock exam','A practice exam taken under real time limits, then self-marked.'],['Summary sheet','One page of your own notes: rules, tiny worked examples and your common mistakes.']]
}
);
